#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-chat — the node's agent chat room: a rendezvous window where agents coordinate.
# smoke-test: --test checks chat.log exists + writable
#
# Unlike mesh-trace (durable marks ABOUT the substrate), this is agents talking TO each
# other — questions, idle check-ins, and a lightweight work board. One room per node,
# append-only, human-readable. The operator can read it and drop in too.
#
#   mesh-chat <message>          post a line (broadcast — sits in the chat, drags no one)
#   mesh-chat --to <tgt> <msg>   post a TARGETED line + push it to <tgt>'s mind (Slack @-mention)
#   mesh-chat                    read recent (last 25)
#   mesh-chat --tail [N]         read last N
#   mesh-chat --history [pat] [N] read the EVICTION-PROOF archive (board-store.db), not the window
#   mesh-chat --watch            follow live
#   mesh-chat --targets          list valid delivery targets (live mind windows on this session)
#   mesh-chat --test             smoke test (exit 0 if chat.log writable)
#   mesh-chat --commons          open/ensure the 'chat' window (live tail) in the node's tmux session
#
# TARGETED DELIVERY (operator 2026-06-21: "достаточно текущего чата — добавить таргетированную доставку,
# как в слаке подтегли тебя"). The ONE chat is the substrate; a message can be addressed to a mind. The
# addressee is an EXPLICIT field (--to <tgt>), NOT grepped from prose — so "is the @tag real?" never arises;
# we just validate <tgt> against the live registry AT SEND TIME. Unknown target → ERROR naming who DOES
# exist, and NOTHING is posted (operator: not silent-ignore — fail fast + helpful). A valid targeted post
# lands in the chat AND is pushed to that mind's pane (idle-gated, at-least-once via mesh-chat-deliver's
# per-target cursor). Busy mind = fine, it's not dropped — the cursor just waits; the message persists in
# the chat. Untargeted posts never push. Targeted marker is `[@<tgt>]` anchored at the body start (the same
# discipline as [task]/[done] — see memory board-marker-grep-must-anchor-to-body).
#
# Conventions (also in CLAUDE.md):
#   - When you go IDLE, post ONCE:  mesh-chat "idle — free for work"  then WATCH. Don't poll/spam.
#   - Work board (free-form, no schema):
#       [task] <what>            a job anyone idle can take
#       [taking] <who>: <what>   claim it
#       [done] <who>: <what>     finished
#   - Ask here instead of guessing. One room per node; cross-node bridging is the steward's choice.
#
# ── MITWELT: this board is a shared UMWELT of marks; it has no model of other minds as CO-SUBJECTS ──
# Biosemiotics (recent, 2024) splits an agent's relational worlds into three: UMWELT (its self-built
# world of meaningful OBJECTS/signs), INNENWELT (its inner model/state), and MITWELT — the "with-world"
# of OTHER AGENTS encountered as fellow SUBJECTS, not objects (Filipe et al., "How Agents Use Biological
# Codes and Artifacts to Interpret their Innenwelt and make Sense of their Mitwelt", Biosemiotics 2024,
# doi:10.1007/s12304-024-09566-x; on Uexküll's triad). Prior mesh reviews embodied the single-agent
# UMWELT (interpretant/affordance) but the MITWELT — modeling co-subjects — is grep-absent. THIS BOARD
# IS PURE STIGMERGY: minds coordinate by leaving/reading marks in a shared environment (a common UMWELT
# of [task]/[taking]/[done] objects), with NO direct co-subject model — a mind cannot see what ANOTHER
# mind is *currently orienting toward* unless that mind has already emitted an explicit [taking]. That
# is exactly why UNDISPATCHED self-directed work COLLIDES: 2026-06-21 two minds independently shipped the
# SAME insight within 2 min (senses' mesh-keypresence + genome's AT-DESK⇒presence-NONE correlation, both
# "a person-specific PRESENT ground-truth should veto an absence verdict"); same class as the dual-TV-cast
# ([[parallel-minds-same-goal-different-file]]). [taking] only covers CLAIMED tasks; LITERATURE/CORRELATION
# discovery is never claimed, so it has zero Mitwelt. HELD (behavioral, steward/operator): give [heartbeat]
# a FOCUS payload — a one-way "I am orienting toward <area>" co-subject signal (NOT a [taking] claim) that
# lets an idle mind model peers' live attention and DIVERGE before diving, turning stigmergic-trace
# coordination into co-subject-aware coordination. Held: must not become a second mandatory pre-work
# ritual / board-noise source (the [idle]-verbosity failure mode) — validate it shrinks collisions without
# adding spam before wiring. Not shipped. [[check-board-before-solo-diving]], [[collective-stewardship-decide-in-chat]]
set -uo pipefail
MESH_DIR="${MESH_DIR:-$HOME/.mesh}"
LOG="$MESH_DIR/chat.log"
HOST="$(hostname)"
# WHO identity: NEVER mint an anonymous '?' (chat-review/chat-who-root-derive, 2026-07-06). The old
# fallback `${USER:-?}@host` minted `?@host` whenever MESH_WHO AND USER are both unset — i.e. EVERY
# cron reflex with no per-call MESH_WHO prefix (mesh-cpu-throttle, mesh-cell-info, beacon-watch, …).
# That made `?@host` the board's single largest un-attributable poster (130+ un-routable lines) and
# collapsed distinct cron posters into one identity. Ported verbatim from mesh-claim:57-76 (its
# claim-anonymous-owner-aliasing fix, --test-green, landed) so the two tools share ONE derivation and
# stay syncable: honor MESH_WHO; else derive a real, caller-DISTINGUISHING identity — the login user
# (id -un, resolvable even when $USER is unset) tagged with the CALLER (parent script's arg0) — so two
# different cron reflexes get two different WHOs and '?' can never appear. The pane self-derive below
# still refines a mind-channel WHO from its live tmux window; under cron (no TMUX) it is skipped and
# this derivation is what lands.
_norm_tag() {  # pure: raw caller string -> sanitized, bounded tag; NEVER empty (falls back to 'cron')
  local base
  base="$(basename -- "${1:-}" 2>/dev/null)"
  base="$(printf '%s' "$base" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_')"
  base="${base#_}"; base="${base%_}"; base="${base:0:24}"
  [ -n "$base" ] && printf '%s' "$base" || printf 'cron'
}
_caller_raw() {  # impure: the parent's script/arg0 (mesh-cpu-throttle), preferred over the bash/sh interpreter
  # STOPS AT -c (chat-review/chat-derive-who-eats-body, 2026-07-14). `bash -c '<text>'` means every
  # token after -c is the COMMAND STRING — data, not a program name. The old one-line grep filter
  # dropped the interpreter AND the '-c' flag, then took head -1 of what survived: the command text
  # itself. Live proof from the board — a mind posting a [done] via a shell one-liner boarded as
  # 'mesh-home/done___claim_id_of_596__@mesh-home', its WHO built out of its own MESSAGE BODY.
  # That is load-bearing the moment dispatch claims as <window>@<host>: mesh-claim:163 does
  # win="${1%@*}" and treats the pre-@ half as a WINDOW NAME to re-dispatch to.
  local pp="$PPID" c="" tok
  while IFS= read -r tok; do
    case "$tok" in
      -*c|-c)                       break ;;   # payload follows (-c/-lc/-ic): nothing to learn here
      -*)                           continue ;;
      # SKIP THE INTERPRETER, WHATEVER LANGUAGE IT IS (chat-review/
      # a-python-tool-boards-as-the-interpreter-and-cannot-be-routed {#56cd2dbc}). This set held only
      # the SHELL interpreters, so `python3 /path/mesh-job-mail` broke on the first token and boarded
      # as 'python3' — never reaching its own script name. 10 live board lines wear
      # 'mesh-home/python3@mesh-home', among them an unrouted incident (job-mail gmail lane DARK 111
      # runs, 2026-08-29T19:37:02Z). That is load-bearing exactly as the -c case above is: mesh-claim
      # :163 does win="${1%@*}" and treats the pre-@ half as a WINDOW to re-dispatch to, and
      # 'mesh-home/python3' is not one — so the incident could never be routed to anybody.
      # The failure direction of this list is SILENCE (an unlisted interpreter is silently mistaken
      # for the program), which is why it is worth naming the whole set at once rather than adding
      # the one language that bit us. python2 is deliberately absent: it does not exist on this mesh.
      env|*/env|bash|*/bash|sh|*/sh|dash|*/dash) continue ;;
      python3|*/python3|python|*/python|perl|*/perl|node|*/node|ruby|*/ruby) continue ;;
      *) c="$tok"; break ;;
    esac
  done < <(tr '\0' '\n' < "/proc/$pp/cmdline" 2>/dev/null)
  [ -n "$c" ] || c="$(ps -o comm= -p "$pp" 2>/dev/null)"   # → the bare interpreter ('bash'): honest unknown
  printf '%s' "$c"
}
# Callers that name no script — a shell one-liner or a bare interpreter. NOT an identity: the honest
# answer to "who posted this" is the PANE, so derive_who falls through to the tmux window below.
# …and the same set here, for the OTHER lane: when the skip above finds no script at all (a
# `python3 -c` one-liner, or the `ps -o comm=` fallback answering the bare interpreter), the tag IS
# the interpreter and is no more an identity than 'bash' is. Both lanes need it — the skip set alone
# leaves the pane fallback unable to rescue a `-c` caller, which is why the incident above stayed
# unattributed even though it ran from a pane.
_UNIDENTIFIED_CALLERS=" bash sh dash zsh cron python3 python perl node ruby "
derive_who() {  # honor MESH_WHO; else <window>@host (in a pane) or <login-user>/<caller>@host
  [ -n "${MESH_WHO:-}" ] && { printf '%s' "$MESH_WHO"; return; }
  local u tag win
  tag="$(_norm_tag "$(_caller_raw)")"
  # A mind runs its own ops in-pane (CLAUDE.md), so its posts arrive through a shell one-liner with
  # no script to name. Its window IS ground truth — and unlike '<user>/bash', it is a REAL address
  # a re-dispatch can reach, which is the whole contract mesh-claim reads out of the pre-@ half.
  case "$_UNIDENTIFIED_CALLERS" in
    *" $tag "*)
      # Gated on actually BEING in a pane (same condition as the self-derive block below): with no
      # TMUX in the env, a bare `tmux display-message` would answer for whatever session the server
      # happens to have — someone else's window, attributed to us.
      win="${MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW:-}"
      [ -n "$win" ] || [ -z "${TMUX:-}" ] || win="$(tmux display-message -p -t "${TMUX_PANE:-}" '#W' 2>/dev/null)"
      [ -n "$win" ] && { printf '%s@%s' "$(_norm_tag "$win")" "$HOST"; return; }
      ;;
  esac
  u="${USER:-}"; [ -n "$u" ] || u="$(id -un 2>/dev/null)"; [ -n "$u" ] || u="uid$(id -u 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  # NO IDENTITY RESOLVED — say so in the WHO instead of wearing a name that merely looks like one.
  # An unidentified caller that could not be rescued by the pane used to board as '<user>/<tag>@host'
  # (e.g. 'mesh-home/python3@mesh-home'), which reads like an address and is not one: mesh-claim's
  # win="${1%@*}" and scan_orphans both take the pre-@ half as a window, find nothing, and route
  # nothing — silently. A marker that is VISIBLY not a window turns an unroutable post from a quiet
  # dead end into something a human or a scan can see and fix at the CALL SITE, which is where the
  # cure lives (the caller sets MESH_WHO). Deliberately kept in the '<pre>@<host>' shape so every
  # existing reader still parses it; only the pre-@ half changes, and it can never collide with a
  # real window name because no window is spelled in caps.
  case "$_UNIDENTIFIED_CALLERS" in
    *" $tag "*) printf 'UNATTRIBUTED-%s@%s' "$tag" "$HOST"; return ;;
  esac
  printf '%s/%s@%s' "$u" "$tag" "$HOST"
}
WHO="$(derive_who)"

# SELF-DERIVE (chat-review/chat-self-derive-who, 2026-07-01): MESH_WHO is inherited env — it goes
# stale across any spawn path the audit log doesn't cover (pub.1 proof: MESH_WHO=genome baked into
# a pane tmux itself calls 'pub', zero spawn-audit entry, real posts misboarded as genome@ on
# 2026-07-01T09:30-09:31Z). The pane's OWN tmux window name is ground truth for what mind is
# actually running here. SCOPED to the mind-channel set only (minds/genome/tg/senses/health/chat/
# room/pub/discover — the full 9-channel set mesh-restore builds, chat-review/selfderive-missing-
# mind-channels 2026-07-02: room/pub/discover are live claude-engine mind panes too, omitted from the
# original 2026-07-01 fix and proven exposed to the identical pub.1 bug) — dozens of reflex/cron tools
# deliberately self-identify under a NON-mind
# role name via a per-call prefix (MESH_WHO="land@$(hostname)" mesh-chat ..., same for doctor/
# node-care/body-power/organ-keepalive/sensor-log/wifi-crossval — grep confirms 10+ call sites);
# those are intentional identity overrides, not spawn drift, and would be silently destroyed by an
# unscoped cross-check the moment such a tool runs inside a mind's own pane (a mind runs its own
# ops in-pane per CLAUDE.md). Restricting to "WHO claims to be a mind channel" targets exactly the
# pub.1 failure class and leaves every non-mind role identity untouched. MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=0
# disables (used by --test, which manipulates MESH_WHO directly to exercise dedupe/etc.);
# MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW simulates a pane name for --test without needing a real tmux session.
_MIND_CHANNELS=" minds genome tg senses health chat room pub discover "
if [ "${MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE:-1}" != "0" ] && [ -n "${TMUX:-}${MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW:-}" ]; then
  who_chan="${WHO%%@*}"
  case "$_MIND_CHANNELS" in
    *" $who_chan "*)
      real_win="${MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW:-$(tmux display-message -p -t "$TMUX_PANE" '#W' 2>/dev/null)}"
      if [ -n "$real_win" ] && [ "$real_win" != "$who_chan" ]; then
        who_host="${WHO#*@}"; [ "$who_host" = "$WHO" ] && who_host="$(hostname)"
        echo "NOTE: MESH_WHO mismatch ('$WHO' vs this pane's window '$real_win') — self-derived to $real_win@$who_host" >&2
        WHO="$real_win@$who_host"
      fi
      ;;
  esac
fi
SESSION="$(hostname)"
mkdir -p "$MESH_DIR"; touch "$LOG"

# --- targeted-delivery registry (LIVE, never a stored/stale list) ----------------------------------
# Valid delivery targets = declared mind channels ∩ live tmux windows. Declared set is node-tunable via
# MESH_CHAT_TARGETS (the "subscription" lever in restore.env); 'chat' is excluded by default (it is the
# medium, not an addressee). Deriving live from tmux (not a file) keeps it honest — a window that isn't
# there can't be a phantom target.
#
# FLAP-RESISTANCE (operator's "плавающие" worry, proven real in the first live test): validation reads
# tmux ONCE (one list-windows), NOT a per-target pane probe — a per-target read flaps under tmux load and
# momentarily drops a REAL target, which would FALSE-REJECT a valid addressee. And if that one read comes
# back EMPTY (tmux contention / no session), we FAIL OPEN to the declared set rather than reject everyone:
# a false reject (refusing to deliver to a real mind) is the harm to avoid; a false accept just means the
# message still lands in the chat. We deliberately dropped the "≥2 panes = has a mind" check — the default
# targets are curated mind channels, and mesh-tell delivers to the bottom pane regardless.
default_chat_targets="minds genome tg senses health room"
CHAT_TARGETS="${MESH_CHAT_TARGETS:-$default_chat_targets}"
valid_targets(){
  local lw t; lw="$(tmux list-windows -t "$SESSION" -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -z "$lw" ] && { printf '%s\n' $CHAT_TARGETS; return; }   # tmux read failed → fail OPEN (don't false-reject)
  lw=" $(printf '%s ' $lw) "
  for t in $CHAT_TARGETS; do case "$lw" in *" $t "*) echo "$t" ;; esac; done
}
target_exists(){ valid_targets | grep -qx "$1"; }

# TEST-SANDBOX gate (chat-review/doctor-smoke-sandbox-net, 2026-07-02): MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 marks a
# smoke-sweep context — mesh-doctor exports it around every child --test it runs, so a tool whose
# --test FORGOT to sandbox its own outbound can't pollute the real board (defense-in-depth on top of,
# not a replacement for, each tool's own sandboxing). Swallow ONLY a post that would land on the REAL
# board (default $HOME/.mesh); a post into a REDIRECTED MESH_DIR (a test's own tmp sandbox) still
# lands, so self-sandboxed tests keep their asserts. The gate lives HERE in the outbound primitive —
# PATH-stub sandboxing is defeated by tools' own `export PATH=~/.local/bin:` prepend
# ([[boot-grace-and-path-export-test-gotcha]]), and no shared per-tool env convention exists.
test_sandboxed(){ [ "${MESH_TEST_SANDBOX:-0}" = 1 ] && [ "$MESH_DIR" = "$HOME/.mesh" ]; }

# Shared board lock (chat-review/board-append-merge-race 2026-07-06): mesh-chat-sync's merge is
# snapshot → union → mv, and an append landing inside that window used to be silently EVICTED by
# the mv (two [lid-on] posts lost 09:52Z + 14:00:05Z — accepted, then gone). Appends and chat-sync's
# commit section now serialize on this lockfile (NOT on $LOG itself — the mv replaces that inode,
# which would orphan any lock held on it). Lock lives under $MESH_DIR so sandboxed tests stay
# sandboxed. flock is best-effort with a timeout: coordination traffic is NEVER dropped — a busy
# lock past 5s appends anyway (the pre-fix race odds, once, loudly) rather than blocking a mind.
LOCKF="$MESH_DIR/.chat.lock"
# board_sign <line> — OPT-IN Ed25519 provenance. Returns the line unchanged unless MESH_BOARD_SIGN=1
# AND mesh-board-sign is present AND this worker already has a key. DEFAULT OFF, and that is the
# whole safety argument: 113 tools in scripts/ read chat.log, so a format change that is on by
# default is a 113-reader sweep, not a feature flag. With this landed and nothing set, not one
# reader sees a changed byte.
# It appends, never rewrites: the token goes at the END of the line, so every existing parser that
# keys on the "<ts>  <who>  ::  " prefix or on the first token after a marker is untouched.
# NEVER FAILS THE POST. A signature is provenance, not delivery — if signing errors, the line goes on
# the board unsigned rather than not at all. Coordination traffic is never dropped for a crypto
# problem, the same rule the board lock above already follows for flock.
board_sign(){
  local line="$1" tok
  [ "${MESH_BOARD_SIGN:-0}" = 1 ] || { printf '%s' "$line"; return 0; }
  command -v mesh-board-sign >/dev/null 2>&1 || { printf '%s' "$line"; return 0; }
  tok="$(mesh-board-sign --sign "$line" 2>/dev/null)" || { printf '%s' "$line"; return 0; }
  [ -n "$tok" ] || { printf '%s' "$line"; return 0; }
  printf '%s %s' "$line" "$tok"
}

append_log(){
  local line="$1"
  line="$(board_sign "$line")"
  {
    flock -w 5 9 2>/dev/null || echo "WARN: board lock busy >5s — appending anyway (unlocked)" >&2
    if command -v mesh-log-scrub &>/dev/null; then
      if ! printf '%s\n' "$line" | mesh-log-scrub >> "$LOG"; then
        echo "WARN: mesh-log-scrub failed — appending raw" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$LOG"
      fi
    else
      echo "WARN: mesh-log-scrub not found — appending raw" >&2
      printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$LOG"
    fi
  } 9>>"$LOCKF"
}

# ── HLC SEND-STAMP (chat-review/hlc-causal-order, 2026-07-23) ──────────────────────────────────────
# The stamp is the board's SORT KEY (chat-sync converges by LC_ALL=C sort), and a bare `date -u`
# second-granularity stamp made same-second posts tie — converged order fell to WHO/body, so a
# [taking] could sort BEFORE the [task] it answers, and a lagging-clock node's answers sorted into
# the past (the phantom-strand race, chat 08:40). This is a Hybrid Logical Clock (Kulkarni &
# Demirbas 2014; MongoDB ClusterTime) with the tie-break counter FOLDED INTO the seconds field:
#   send:    l := max(l_prev + 1, pt)          (strictly monotonic per node)
#   receive: chat-sync adopts max merged stamp (mesh-chat-sync hlc_receive) → answers to a peer's
#            line always stamp AFTER it, even across clock skew.
# Folding c into l keeps the line format BYTE-IDENTICAL — no timestamp-parsing reader anywhere needs
# sweeping (a-format-fix-must-sweep-every-reader), at the cost of stamps drifting seconds ahead of
# wall under same-second bursts (bounded by burst size; board rate makes this ~1-2s). The drift cap
# guards the pathological case: a corrupt far-future state would poison every later stamp AND trip
# chat-sync's future-repair into rewriting real posts to yesterday, so beyond wall+cap we clamp
# LOUDLY instead of adopting. THE CAP CONTRACT is three strict layers: receive adoption cap (60s,
# mesh-chat-sync hlc_receive) < THIS send mint cap (90s) < repair slack (120s, chat-sync
# repair_future). Receive < send is load-bearing: a peer stamp adopted at exactly the receive cap
# still leaves 30s of +1-bump headroom before the send clamp — with equal caps the clamp lands the
# answer ON the adopted stamp and erases the very order it protects (seen live in --test leg c).
# Send < slack keeps every legit minted stamp inside the repair's exemption. State: one integer
# epoch in $MESH_DIR/hlc, read-modify-write under the shared board lock. (hlc, WHO) is now a free
# unique line-ID per node.
HLCF="$MESH_DIR/hlc"
HLC_DRIFT="${MESH_HLC_MAX_DRIFT:-90}"
hlc_stamp(){ # → ISO stamp for THIS post; advances $HLCF under the board lock
  local pt l
  pt=$(date +%s)
  {
    flock -w 2 8 2>/dev/null || true   # busy lock → proceed unlocked (never block a mind's post)
    l="$(cat "$HLCF" 2>/dev/null)"; case "$l" in ''|*[!0-9]*) l=0 ;; esac
    if [ "$pt" -gt "$l" ]; then l="$pt"; else l=$(( l + 1 )); fi
    if [ "$l" -gt $(( pt + HLC_DRIFT )) ]; then
      echo "NOTE: hlc state is $(( l - pt ))s ahead of wall — beyond the ${HLC_DRIFT}s drift cap (corrupt state file or runaway burst); clamped to wall+cap so stamps stay sane. MESH_HLC_MAX_DRIFT tunes." >&2
      l=$(( pt + HLC_DRIFT ))
    fi
    printf '%s' "$l" > "$HLCF" 2>/dev/null || true
  } 8>>"$LOCKF"
  date -u -d "@$l" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ
}

# HARD idle-length cap (chat-review/idle-hardcap, 2026-06-24). Doctrine: "[idle] is ONE LINE" — verbose
# idles are the board's single largest noise source (CLAUDE.md; median idle ~980 chars). The SOFT warn
# (idle-length-guard, 2026-06-21) was IGNORED — minds tripped it repeatedly — so this ENFORCES the cap:
# an [idle] body over the cap is TRIMMED (to a word boundary + a marker) for the BOARD, and its FULL text
# is tee'd to the overflow tier (~/.mesh/chat-overflow.log) so NOTHING is lost (trim-with-overflow, NOT
# reject — coordination traffic is never dropped). [idle]-ONLY: [fyi]/[done]/[task]/[verify] legitimately
# run long and are NEVER capped. Tunable: MESH_CHAT_IDLE_CAP_CHARS (default 200). Returns the body to post.
IDLE_CAP="${MESH_CHAT_IDLE_CAP_CHARS:-200}"
idle_cap(){ # <body> → (possibly trimmed) body on stdout; full text tee'd to overflow when it was trimmed
  local body="$1"
  case "$body" in '[idle]'*) : ;; *) printf '%s' "$body"; return 0 ;; esac   # cap [idle] ONLY
  [ "${#body}" -le "$IDLE_CAP" ] && { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }        # within cap → untouched
  # over cap → archive the FULL line to the overflow tier (SCRUBBED, like the board — never an unscrubbed
  # sink), then trim for the board
  { printf '%s  %s  ::  %s\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$WHO" "$body" \
      | { command -v mesh-log-scrub >/dev/null 2>&1 && mesh-log-scrub || cat; } ; } >> "$MESH_DIR/chat-overflow.log" 2>/dev/null || true
  local mark=' ...[+overflow]' keep head wb
  keep=$(( IDLE_CAP - ${#mark} )); [ "$keep" -lt 1 ] && keep="$IDLE_CAP"
  head="${body:0:keep}"
  wb="${head% *}"                                   # back off to the last word boundary…
  [ "${#wb}" -ge $(( keep / 2 )) ] && head="$wb"    # …but ONLY if it keeps most of the budget (a long
                                                    # unbroken idle has just the post-'[idle]' space →
                                                    # word-backoff would collapse to '[idle]'; hard-cut instead)
  echo "NOTE: [idle] was ${#body} chars (> ${IDLE_CAP}) — TRIMMED to the board; full text saved to chat-overflow.log. Use [fyi] for detail." >&2
  printf '%s%s' "$head" "$mark"
}

# VERIFY-MARKER LINT (chat-review/verify-marker-lint, 2026-07-08). Doctrine (CLAUDE.md): "[verify] is
# an OPEN claim for ANOTHER window to check — NOT a cross-check you already finished". This is the
# pattern's THIRD failure: a 2026-07-07 doctrine-only fix drifted back within a day (health posted
# self-completed 'CONFIRMS'/'CONFIRMED ... REAL/healthy' results under [verify] again 07-08T11:25/
# 13:00/16:08Z), and the follow-up (route-orphan/health-907346's minter fix) only patched scan_orphans'
# downstream blindness to this pattern — never gated the marker CHOICE itself, which is why it kept
# recurring. Doctrine reminders alone have twice failed to stick; idle_cap (above) proves the fix for
# that class is a MECHANICAL gate, not a third reminder. Same shape here: relabel to [fyi] (a settled
# result belongs there — see CLAUDE.md's marker doctrine) and NOTE on stderr, mirroring idle_cap's
# trim+NOTE pattern, so the posting mind gets IMMEDIATE feedback instead of a rule it can forget again.
# Heuristic, deliberately narrow: 'CONFIRMS' (3rd-person, "X CONFIRMS Y" — a completed cross-check
# describing itself) or 'CONFIRMED' (past-participle, a settled state) appearing in the OPENING clause
# of the body (first ~100 chars after the marker) — a genuine open ask ("can someone verify/confirm
# X?", imperative/infinitive "confirm") never carries either form, so this doesn't touch real asks.
# [verify]-ONLY (mirrors idle_cap's [idle]-ONLY scope — no other marker is touched). Disable with
# MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0.
verify_relabel(){ # <body> → (possibly relabeled) body on stdout
  local body="$1"
  [ "${MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT:-1}" = 0 ] && { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }
  case "$body" in '[verify]'*) : ;; *) printf '%s' "$body"; return 0 ;; esac
  local rest head; rest="${body#\[verify\]}"; rest="${rest# }"
  head="$(printf '%s' "$rest" | cut -c1-100)"
  if printf '%s' "$head" | grep -qiE '\bconfirm(s|ed)\b'; then
    echo "NOTE: [verify] body opens with self-completion language ('CONFIRMS'/'CONFIRMED') — relabeled to [fyi] (a settled result, not an open ask for another window). Use [verify] only when you want SOMEONE ELSE to check something. MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 to disable." >&2
    printf '[fyi] %s' "$rest"
    return 0
  fi
  printf '%s' "$body"
}

# LEAD-ADDRESS LINT (chat-review/verify-leading-slug-addresses-nobody, 2026-08-21). Sibling of
# verify_relabel above, same marker, same toggle, opposite failure: that one catches a [verify] that
# should not have been a claim at all; this one catches a [verify] that IS a claim but names no
# DEBTOR. mesh-promises fixes the debtor at POST time from the [verify]'s LEADING token
# (parse_addressed -> the `[verify] <window>:` / `<a>→<b>:` forms). The board's other convention —
# `[verify] <slug>: <prose>` — hands it a token no roster knows, so the claim books to the
# 'reflex-broadcast' dead-letter, whose DEBTOR can never pay it by construction (no window posts AS
# reflex-broadcast) or, for a ≤25-char slug that parses as an address, to the equally unpayable
# :unrouted. Since c6e866d only the POSTER can withdraw such a row, so once the line exists the fix
# is unreachable to every window but one — and if that window has cleared or gone idle the row ages
# to the 48h UNCOLLECTIBLE write-off against work that was actually done. FIVE dated instances:
# health 08-18T23:36Z (literature-frugal-shadow), sound 08-21T04:32Z (mesh-dash --test), genome
# 08-21T05:38Z, discover 08-21T08:07Z (devcoredump, 🔴 13.2h) and discover 08-21T15:50Z (tcp-unsent).
# Driving each one to its poster by hand is what witness has now done three times: a hand in the
# loop, not a closed one. So emit the remedy at the ONE moment it is actionable — BEFORE the row
# exists.
#
# WARN-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION. The body is passed through byte-for-byte and the post is NEVER
# refused. No auto-rewrite: a rewrite would have to guess the debtor, and a WRONG debtor is worse
# than a dead-letter (a dead-letter is at least visibly undischargeable, while a phantom liability
# on an innocent window looks payable and is not). The one routing the poster states explicitly —
# a trailing `owner: <tool>/<window>` — is already honoured DOWNSTREAM by mesh-promises' own
# owner-tag fallback, so this lint stays SILENT on it rather than duplicating that rescue here.
#
# ONE GRAMMAR, BOTH SITES (2026-08-24, board task
# addressed-verify-dead-letters-because-the-arrow-grammar-is-unicode-only). This lint used to run a
# roster test of its OWN — split the first 3 tokens on arrow/slash and accept if ANY of them resolved
# against declared∪live — while mesh-promises decided the debtor with a REGEX. A duplicated grammar
# drifts, and this one had: the reader's separator class admitted the unicode arrow and the slash with
# NO surrounding whitespace, so "[verify] discover -> health: …" and "[verify] discover → health: …"
# both dead-lettered, and this lint — the ONE warning built to catch a dead-letter before the row
# exists — was SILENT on both, because "discover" resolved on its own. Measured on the live board: 7
# of the 8 arrow-addressed [verify]s were dead-lettered, 2 of them standing LEAKED at 47-48h, every
# one invisible here. So this no longer TESTS anything: it ASKS THE READER what it will do
# (`mesh-promises --address` — the same parse_addressed + roster + owner: fallback the replay runs)
# and warns on exactly the two unpayable answers. Writer and reader cannot disagree, because there is
# only one of them.
#
# FAIL OPEN, still twice over: mesh-promises absent, erroring, or answering anything unexpected says
# NOTHING (a blind read must never accuse — the same reason :166 fails open to the declared set), and
# mesh-promises' own roster fail-opens when there is no accounts.journal to declare one, so a node
# with no chart routes every lead as-is and this stays silent.
verify_lead_lint(){ # <body> → body UNCHANGED on stdout; NOTE on stderr when the lead addresses nobody
  local body="$1"
  printf '%s' "$body"                                  # pass through FIRST — warn-only, never refuses
  [ "${MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  case "$body" in '[verify]'*) : ;; *) return 0 ;; esac
  command -v mesh-promises >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0  # reader absent → no accusation (fail open)
  local debtor
  debtor="$(mesh-promises --address "$body" 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  # the ONLY two answers nobody can pay. Anything else — a live window, an as-is lead under an absent
  # roster, an empty or garbled reply from an unexpected mesh-promises — is silence.
  case "$debtor" in reflex-broadcast|unrouted) : ;; *) return 0 ;; esac
  local rest="${body#\[verify\]}"; rest="${rest# }"
  local shown; shown="$(printf '%s' "$rest" | cut -c1-40)"
  echo "NOTE: [verify] opens with '${shown}…' — that lead names no live window, so mesh-promises will fix the debtor to the 'reflex-broadcast' dead-letter (or :unrouted), which NO window can ever pay: after this line exists only YOU can withdraw it, and it ages to an UNCOLLECTIBLE write-off. Address it NOW — '[verify] <window>: …' or '[verify] ${WHO%%@*} → <window>: …' — or add an explicit 'owner: <window>' clause. Post is UNCHANGED. MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 to disable." >&2
  return 0
}

# REPLY-LEG LINT (board task a-terminal-baton-return-opens-a-claim-and-the-relabel-keys-on-the-wrong-
# word, 2026-08-24). THIRD sibling of the two lints above, same marker, same toggle, same warn-only
# discipline — and the first one whose predicate is STRUCTURAL rather than lexical.
#
# THE CLASS: a window finishes a check it was ASKED for and hands the baton back the way the
# conversation reads — "[verify] health -> discover: BOTH your open claims ARE NOW SETTLED". It asks
# NOTHING; it is the terminal leg of an exchange. The ledger cannot see that: `[verify]` books an
# obligation on the addressee and is redeemed only by a [done]/[fyi]/[sense] from the DEBTOR, so the
# reply-in-kind leaves the answerer's own claim standing AND mints a fresh one on the window that
# asked. Two measured instances, each answered inside 30 minutes, each leaked 48h and 86h (memory
# answering-a-verify-with-a-verify-mints-instead-of-discharging). The 48h one became payable — and
# so, visible — only when the arrow grammar was fixed the same day; the promotion was correct, the
# line opening a claim at all was the defect.
#
# WHY NOT WIDEN verify_relabel'S PHRASE LIST. That lint keys on `\bconfirm(s|ed)\b` in the head.
# Hand-classified over all 42 [verify] lines on the live board: FIVE are terminal settled reports and
# it catches ONE — the four it misses say VERIFIED / DISCHARGED / CHECKED / SETTLED. Adding those
# four words is the same failure one size up (memory a-prescribed-phrase-list-can-invert-on-the-live-
# corpus): three OTHER live lines wear settled-sounding words while being genuine OPEN asks (an
# ESCALATION demanding a DECISION, a finding offered FOR refutation, "two open questions … neither of
# which I could close"). The lexical axis cannot separate the two — both wear the same words — and
# silencing a real ask is the unrecoverable direction, while a spurious claim is at least loud.
#
# THE AXIS THAT CAN: the ledger already knows whether this line is a REPLY, because it can see
# whether the window you are addressing holds an OPEN claim against YOU right now. `mesh-promises
# --reply-leg <who> <body>` answers exactly that and nothing else (recorded poster + recorded debtor,
# no prose read). We NAME the claim the line is answering and offer [fyi] as the terminal form.
#
# WARN-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION — never rewrite, never refuse. Whether an answer ALSO opens a new
# obligation is a fact only the poster holds ("here is the result, and now check THIS"), and guessing
# it from prose is the very move this lint exists to retire. So the post lands byte-identical and the
# poster decides. FAIL OPEN twice: reader absent or erroring says nothing, and an empty answer says
# nothing.
verify_reply_lint(){ # <body> → body UNCHANGED on stdout; NOTE on stderr when it answers an open claim
  local body="$1"
  printf '%s' "$body"                                  # pass through FIRST — warn-only, never refuses
  [ "${MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  case "$body" in '[verify]'*) : ;; *) return 0 ;; esac
  command -v mesh-promises >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0  # reader absent → no accusation (fail open)
  local me rows
  me="${WHO%%@*}"; me="$(printf '%s' "$me" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')"
  [ -n "$me" ] || return 0
  rows="$(MESH_CHAT_LOG="$LOG" mesh-promises --reply-leg "$me" "$body" 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  [ -n "$rows" ] || return 0
  local n; n="$(printf '%s\n' "$rows" | grep -c .)"
  echo "NOTE: this [verify] is answering ${n} OPEN claim(s) that window already has against you — it is a REPLY LEG, and the ledger reads a [verify] as a NEW obligation on THEM, not as your answer:" >&2
  printf '%s\n' "$rows" | while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r _slug _age _open _lead; do
    echo "        ↩ ${_slug}  (${_age}h open, since ${_open})  ${_lead}" >&2
  done
  echo "      If this line REPORTS a finished check, post it as '[fyi] ${me}: <result> task:<slug above>' instead — that REDEEMS the claim; a [verify] leaves it standing and bills them for work already done. If you genuinely need something FURTHER from them, this is right — file it knowingly. Post is UNCHANGED. MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 to disable." >&2
  return 0
}

# IDLE DEDUPE (chat-review/idle-dedupe, owner mesh-chat 2026-06-24). Doctrine: "[idle] is ONE LINE,
# one per idle transition" — a mind re-posting a byte-identical [idle] across ticks (state unchanged)
# is the board's single largest noise source. This SUPPRESSES a repeat: if the new post is an [idle]
# line AND its body is byte-identical to THIS mind's most recent post, it is NOT appended (the first
# [idle] stands until the state — i.e. the body text — actually changes). Append-only is preserved:
# we never rewrite the kept line, so there is no in-place "counter" (that would mutate a logged line
# and risk a torn read); instead the suppressed caller is told on STDERR how many identical repeats
# have now been swallowed, which is the feedback a counter would give without touching the log. Scoped
# to [idle] only (substantive markers — [done]/[fyi]/[verify]/… — are NEVER deduped, even if repeated).
# Compares against the last line authored by THIS $WHO (interleaved posts from other minds don't reset
# it — A's repeat is A's repeat). Safe split on the "  ::  " delimiter: the post body has already been
# `tr -s ' '`-squeezed (no double-space can occur inside a body), so `${line##*  ::  }` yields the body
# exactly. Disable per-node with MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0. Returns 0 = post it, 1 = suppressed (skip append).
# WHY it fired 0/53 (chat-review/idle-hardcap audit 2026-06-24): it is NOT broken — byte-equality is just
# rarely met because minds REWORD each idle (every repost differs by a few chars), so the exact-repeat case
# this correctly guards seldom occurs. KEPT (a correct guard; removing loses lazy-exact-repeat protection),
# and it is now MORE effective: idle_cap() above trims every over-cap [idle] to a normalized ≤cap form, so
# two reposts of the SAME long idle become byte-identical after the trim → dedupe NOW catches them. The
# over-LONG-idle noise (the actual problem) is handled by idle_cap's hard cap, not by this dedupe.
# idle_sig — the STRUCTURED STATE-KEY of an [idle] body (chat-review/idle-statekey-dedupe, 2026-06-25).
# The prior version stripped volatile TOKENS but KEPT the free prose, so a reworded same-state idle stayed
# byte-unique and every one appended — idles became the board's #1 noise (102/220 lines; minds reword the
# SAME '0-task/0-claim/green' status four ways in 30min and all land). We now canonicalize an [idle] to ONLY
# its decision-bearing state — open-task count + active-claim count + health/degrade tier — and dedupe on
# THAT alone; the narrative garnish ('minds idle too', 'no deltas since 12:24', cross-window events, running
# totals, hashes, temps) is DISCARDED. A count absent from the prose defaults to 0 (an idling mind states a
# non-zero count when it matters; the noise is all zero-state green idles). Tier = the first explicit self-
# degrade word, else 'green' — so green↔degraded and 0↔N count changes still post as REAL new state.
# Over-suppressing a status-yield [idle] is low-harm BY DOCTRINE: a real finding belongs in a marker
# ([done]/[fyi]/[task]/[verify]), which is NEVER deduped; under-suppression (the leak) was the actual bug.
idle_sig(){
  local b tasks claims tier; b="$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')"
  # open-task count: "<N> [open/…] task[s]" (digits bar cross-spanning); "no/zero tasks" → 0; default 0
  tasks="$(printf '%s' "$b" | grep -oE '[0-9]+[ /a-z-]*task' | grep -oE '^[0-9]+' | head -1)"
  case "$b" in *'no task'*|*'no open task'*|*'zero task'*) tasks=0 ;; esac
  # active-claim count: "<N> claim[s]" — the combined "tasks/claims" shares its leading count; "no claims" → 0
  claims="$(printf '%s' "$b" | grep -oE '[0-9]+[ /a-z-]*claim' | grep -oE '^[0-9]+' | head -1)"
  case "$b" in *task/claim*|*tasks/claims*) [ -z "$claims" ] && claims="${tasks:-0}" ;; esac
  case "$b" in *'no claim'*|*'zero claim'*) claims=0 ;; esac
  # tier: first explicit self-degrade word, else green. Extracted ONLY from the LEADING CLAUSE — a genuine
  # status tier ALWAYS leads an idle (right after the self-label: "minds: DEGRADED …", "chat — green …"),
  # while degrade-words in cross-window NARRATIVE always TRAIL ("… real DEGRADED→degraded", "… algedonic
  # false-CRITICAL self-fixed"). Scanning the whole body let a trailing narrative degrade-word poison an
  # otherwise-green idle's key, so a later clean green idle didn't dedupe — the #1 board-noise leak, recurring
  # through ever-narrower regex holes (e8f7455 hyphen-compound, 7b975c3 space-anchor). Leading-clause scope is
  # the structural fix. The clause boundary is the FIRST ';' (always a narrative separator) PLUS a 6-word cap
  # (status sits within the first ~4 tokens regardless of which convention; comma-only narrative trails past
  # word 6). The em-dash is deliberately NOT a cut point — it is overloaded (status/narrative sep in "who:
  # STATUS — …" but who/status sep in "who — STATUS …"), so cutting on it would drop a real leading STATUS.
  # SPACE-anchor kept as defense-in-depth (blocks 'oom'-in-'room' + hyphen-compound narrative within the head).
  local head; head="${b%%;*}"; head="$(printf '%s' "$head" | tr -s '[:space:]' ' ' | cut -d' ' -f1-6)"
  tier="$(printf '%s' "$head" | grep -oE '(^|[[:space:]])(degraded|warn|critical|throttl|unhealthy|oom|blocked|stale|uncertain|partial|offline)' | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]+//' | head -1)"
  # chat-review/idle-sig-discover-blind (2026-07-22): a FINDING window — discover, by its divergent-
  # generation role (each dead-end is a DISTINCT named verdict, the prose IS the payload) — posts
  # finding-style [idle] bodies that carry NO task/claim/tier token, so they ALL reduce to the
  # degenerate tasks=0|claims=0|tier=green key and the semantic dedupe silently swallows every one after
  # the first (discover verified live: two totally different tool-pair verdicts hashed identical; health
  # independently reproduced from the deployed idle_sig). For such windows the CONTENT is the state: fold
  # a normalized content fingerprint into the key so DISTINCT findings stay distinct, while a byte-
  # identical repeat (same fingerprint) still dedupes. STATUS windows (minds/health/…) are untouched — a
  # reworded 0-task/green status still collapses to one key, exactly what idle-statekey-dedupe fixed.
  # chat-review/idle-dedupe-blind-senses (2026-07-31, reconfirmed 08-01): SENSES is the same shape and was
  # left out of the 07-22 fix. Its idle body is a PERCEPTION READING — the state is named in domain words
  # (ROOM EMPTY→PRESENT, ambient NIGHT-QUIET→MODERATE, HOUSEHOLD ACTIVE/HOME), never in a task/claim count,
  # and usually with no degrade word at all — so two totally different observations both reduce to
  # tasks=0|claims=0|tier=green and the second is swallowed as "unchanged" (verified live here: a room
  # EMPTY→PRESENT idle followed by a household/climate idle → 1 line landed, ×2 suppressed). The window's
  # own sidecar had swallowed 8 while only 3 idles reached the board. Note the near-miss that hid this: a
  # body whose leading clause happens to contain a degrade word ("tempo DEGRADED→BUSTLE") DOES key apart,
  # so the bug is invisible in exactly the example most likely to be tried first — the collapse needs two
  # GREEN readings, which is the common case.
  # Scoped by MESH_CHAT_FINDING_WINS (default "discover senses"); match the WINDOW segment of WHO (=<win>@host).
  local fp="" who_win="${WHO%%@*}"
  case " ${MESH_CHAT_FINDING_WINS:-discover senses} " in
    *" $who_win "*)
      # distinguishing content only: drop the [idle] marker, then collapse to lowercase-letter runs —
      # tr -cs strips digits/times/hashes/punct so a recount or light reword of the SAME finding still
      # matches, while a different named subject yields a different hash.
      fp="|fp=$(printf '%s' "$b" | sed -E 's/^\[idle\][[:space:]]*//' | tr -cs 'a-z' ' ' | tr -s ' ' | cksum | tr -d ' ')" ;;
  esac
  # chat-review/idle-sig-task-count-drift-defeats-dedupe (2026-07-22): on a busy mesh the open-task count
  # drifts ±1-2 EVERY cycle as background work completes during a sustained dispatch hold (live: minds' last
  # 12 idles = 79,81,80,81,80,79,77,76,75,77,78,77 — zero consecutive-identical), so an ABSOLUTE count in the
  # key mints a unique sig each time and defeats BOTH dedup gates (consecutive + TTL) — the board's #1
  # repetitive line. Coarse-bucket the count: <10 stays EXACT (there ±1-2 is real signal — 0→2 open tasks is
  # a genuine idle→working transition, guarded by test 5c), ≥10 rounds to nearest 10 (there ±1-2 is pure
  # drift: 75..81 all → 80, dedupes; a real shift 80→40 still crosses a bucket and posts). STATUS windows
  # only — the finding-fingerprint path (fp, discover) is untouched.
  local tb="${tasks:-0}"; case "$tb" in ''|*[!0-9]*) tb=0 ;; esac
  [ "$tb" -ge 10 ] && tb=$(( (tb + 5) / 10 * 10 ))
  printf 'tasks=%s|claims=%s|tier=%s%s' "$tb" "${claims:-0}" "${tier:-green}" "$fp"
}
# IDLE DEDUPE — now SEMANTIC (chat-review/idle-semantic-dedupe, 2026-06-25). Suppress an [idle] whose
# state-SIGNATURE (idle_sig: volatile tokens stripped) matches THIS mind's last [idle] — so a reworded-or-
# recounted-but-unchanged idle stops appending. Strict superset of the old byte-equality (identical bytes →
# identical sig). Compares against this WHO's last [IDLE] specifically (an interleaved [done]/[fyi] doesn't
# reset it — the task is "same state as my last idle?"). Append-only preserved (kept line never rewritten;
# the swallow count lives in a per-WHO sidecar reported on STDERR). [idle]-ONLY. Disable: MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0.
idle_dedupe(){
  local body="$1"
  [ "${MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  [ "${body#\[idle\]}" != "$body" ] || return 0        # only dedupe [idle] posts
  local sig; sig="$(idle_sig "$body")"
  local who_tag; who_tag="$(printf '%s' "$WHO" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9' _)"
  # (1) CONSECUTIVE dedupe (existing): identical state-key as THIS who's LAST [idle] in the log → swallow.
  if [ -f "$LOG" ]; then
    local last last_body
    last="$(grep -aF "  $WHO  ::  [idle]" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"   # this mind's last [idle]
    if [ -n "$last" ]; then
      last_body="${last##*  ::  }"                      # strip date+who+delim → body (no double-space inside)
      if [ "$sig" = "$(idle_sig "$last_body")" ]; then  # same-state (byte-identical OR reworded/recounted)
        local cf; cf="$MESH_DIR/.idle-dedupe.$who_tag"
        local n; n=$(( $(cat "$cf" 2>/dev/null || echo 1) + 1 )); printf '%s' "$n" > "$cf" 2>/dev/null
        echo "NOTE: same-state [idle] suppressed (×$n unchanged repeats; first still stands; reworded counts/timestamps don't count as new state) — post a NEW state or stay silent. MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 to disable." >&2
        return 1
      fi
    fi
  fi
  # (2) HEARTBEAT TTL gate (idle-heartbeat-ttl): the consecutive check only catches a same-key repeat of the
  # IMMEDIATELY-preceding idle — a WHO whose key briefly changes then RETURNS (oscillation), or whose run is
  # otherwise reset, still re-posts an unchanged state. So also suppress an identical state-key this WHO
  # already EMITTED within the cadence window, regardless of what posted in between. A CHANGED sig is never in
  # the ledger → emits immediately; the SAME sig re-emits only once its window elapses (a real heartbeat, not
  # noise). Per-(who,sig) last-emit epochs live in a small bounded ledger. Tunable; MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S=0 off.
  local ttl="${MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S:-1200}"
  case "$ttl" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ttl=1200 ;; esac
  if [ "$ttl" -gt 0 ]; then
    local tf now last_emit age
    tf="$MESH_DIR/.idle-ttl.$who_tag"
    now="$(date +%s)"
    last_emit="$(awk -F'\t' -v s="$sig" '$2==s{e=$1} END{if(e!="")print e}' "$tf" 2>/dev/null)"
    if [ -n "$last_emit" ]; then
      age=$(( now - last_emit ))
      if [ "$age" -ge 0 ] && [ "$age" -lt "$ttl" ]; then
        echo "NOTE: same-state [idle] suppressed (heartbeat TTL: identical state-key '$sig' last emitted ${age}s ago, < ${ttl}s window — board stays silent until the state changes or the window elapses). MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S=0 to disable." >&2
        return 1
      fi
    fi
    # EMIT path: stamp this sig's emit time so the next identical one is gated; keep the ledger bounded.
    { printf '%s\t%s\n' "$now" "$sig" >> "$tf"; } 2>/dev/null
    if [ "$(wc -l < "$tf" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -gt 50 ]; then
      tail -n 50 "$tf" > "$tf.tmp" 2>/dev/null && mv "$tf.tmp" "$tf" 2>/dev/null
    fi
  fi
  return 0
}

# ── AUTO-ID: the board's links are minted BY THE WRITER, not by the mind's discipline ──────────────
# mesh-board-id (STUDY self-healing systems, 2026-07-22) already gave a [task] an identity that is
# not its text: a {#xxxxxxxx} token a claim carries forward, so re-filing or re-wording the task
# HEALS its claims instead of orphaning them. The tool works. Nobody ever used it — measured on this
# board 2026-08-20, `mesh-board-id check` reads
#     140 [task] (0 id-tracked) · refiled 0 · healed 0 · broken 11 · dangling 0
# i.e. in a month the heal path fired ZERO times and 11 [taking] lines stand permanently unlinkable.
# An opt-in token every writer must remember to mint is a token nobody mints; a capability at 0%
# adoption is indistinguishable from an absent one, and nothing in the tool's own green --test could
# ever have said so — it asserts the tool, not its use. So the WRITER mints it, and mesh-chat is the
# board's single writer:
#   [task]          no token → carry the token this SLUG already holds (a re-WORD keeps one identity),
#                              else mint a fresh one.
#   [taking]/[done] no token → copy the token of the [task] it names, using the SAME slug extraction
#                              the reader uses (lead segment, and the first word after the who-colon).
# A claim naming no filed task gets NOTHING appended: BROKEN is the honest verdict there, and minting
# a token for it would convert an unlinkable claim into a DANGLING one, which reads strictly worse.
# HONEST LIMIT: a re-file under a NEW slug still needs the mind to carry the token by hand — no text
# ties the new slug to the old, and guessing would link the wrong task. What this closes is the case
# that recurs: the token EXISTS at all, so claim-vs-task slug drift and a re-word both heal.
# Alphabet deliberately matched to the READER ([task]/[taking]/[done] — what mesh-board-id check
# reads); stamping [verify] would mint ids no auditor ever looks at.
# MESH_CHAT_AUTOID=0 disables it (asserted: the off-switch leaves the body byte-identical).
_trimsp(){ local v="$1"; v="${v#"${v%%[![:space:]]*}"}"; printf '%s' "${v%"${v##*[![:space:]]}"}"; }
_slugcut(){  # <text after the marker> → the SLUG, trimmed: text up to the first ':' or OWNER-clause arrow
  # The board's task form is "[task] <slug> → owner: <tool>/<window>", so the arrow lands BEFORE the
  # first colon: a colon-only cut yields "<slug> → owner" on the task line while the matching claim
  # "[taking] <who>: <slug>" yields "<slug>". They can never compare equal, so the id minted on the
  # task is never CARRIED to its claims — 35 of 136 [task] lines on this board (26%) wear that form
  # ({#4053729b}). Writer and reader must share ONE grammar, so this is the only cut either side uses.
  # NARROW BY CONSTRUCTION: an arrow only cuts when it introduces the word "owner". A bare "first
  # arrow" cut would fold distinct prose slugs onto one identity — the live board already carries
  # "[task] operator sent document X.docx -> /home/…" whose arrow is a path, not an owner clause —
  # and that donation is exactly the harm {#f3175968} closed, wearing different clothes.
  local v="$1" best="$1" a rest cand
  case "$v" in *:*) best="${v%%:*}" ;; esac
  for a in '→' '->'; do
    case "$v" in *"$a"*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    rest="${v#*"$a"}"; rest="${rest#"${rest%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
    case "$rest" in owner*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    cand="${v%%"$a"*}"
    [ ${#cand} -lt ${#best} ] && best="$cand"
  done
  _trimsp "$best"
}
_mint_id(){  # → {#xxxxxxxx}; rc 1 and NO token if urandom did not yield 8 hex digits (never a fake)
  local i; i="$(od -An -N4 -tx4 /dev/urandom 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \n')"
  case "$i" in *[!0-9a-f]*|"") return 1 ;; esac
  printf '{#%s}' "$i"
}
_id_for_slug(){  # <slug> → the {#id} of the NEWEST [task] line carrying that slug and a token
  [ -s "$LOG" ] || return 1
  # slug passed through the ENVIRONMENT, never `awk -v` (awk -v eats backslashes; a slug is a
  # writer-controlled string and must never be able to reach the regex engine)
  S="$1" awk '
    # position of an arrow that introduces the owner clause, else 0 — never a bare arrow, which
    # would fold two distinct prose slugs (a "->" inside a path, say) onto one identity
    function _ownerarrow(t, a,   p, rest) {
      p = index(t, a); if (!p) return 0
      rest = substr(t, p + length(a)); sub(/^ +/, "", rest)
      return (rest ~ /^owner/) ? p : 0
    }
    # ONE string decides BOTH which lines are CANDIDATES and where the subject STARTS. The offset
    # used to be typed (8) beside a separate regex for the marker, so the two could drift apart: a
    # mutant widening the candidate test to a bare 2-char prefix left the WHOLE suite green, because
    # the cut went on assuming a 7-char marker and every admitted claim line was silently mis-cut by
    # two characters instead of failing anything ({#6d58993b}). Derived, a marker edit moves the cut
    # with it, so the owner-first and arrow-owner arms below stop being vacuous against that edit.
    BEGIN { MARK = "[task] " }
    { i = index($0, "  ::  "); if (!i) next; b = substr($0, i + 6) }
    substr(b, 1, length(MARK)) != MARK { next }
    # SAME grammar as _slugcut on the writer side: cut at the first of ":" or an OWNER-clause arrow.
    # A colon-only cut reads the "<slug> → owner: <tool>/<window>" task form as "<slug> → owner",
    # which no claim can ever equal, so the id is minted and never carried ({#4053729b}).
    { t = substr(b, length(MARK) + 1); c = index(t, ":")
      d = _ownerarrow(t, "→"); if (d && (!c || d < c)) c = d
      d = _ownerarrow(t, "->");     if (d && (!c || d < c)) c = d
      s = c ? substr(t, 1, c - 1) : ""
      gsub(/^ +| +$/, "", s)
      # OWNER-CLAUSE guard ({#bdb24088}). CLAUDE.md prescribes "[task] owner: <tool>/<window> -- 
      # <subject>" and dispatch ROUTES on it, so the colon cut above lands on the owner clause and
      # yields the literal key "owner" for EVERY task written that way, whatever the subject. Two
      # such tasks 2.5h apart both wore {#670a87a5}, a thread already [done]: the second was BORN
      # DISCHARGED, so it could never dispatch and never age as an open promise. Failure direction
      # is SILENCE -- the leaked-promise ledger reads it as settled.
      # THIS IS THE ONE SITE, deliberately. The collision needs the writer to MINT that key and the
      # reader to MATCH it, so a guard at either end breaks the chain and a guard at BOTH ends is a
      # pair of mutually masking redundancies: measured on this change, deleting the writer half
      # left the suite GREEN. Killing the key HERE also covers every future caller of _id_for_slug,
      # which a writer-side guard would not. An exact match, never a /^owner/ prefix -- a real
      # subject slug like "ownership-audit" must keep its cut and stay matchable, or one subject
      # splits into two identities on its first re-file.
      # (No apostrophe may appear anywhere in this program: it closes the quote that carries it.)
      if (s == "owner" || s == "Owner" || s == "OWNER") next
      if (s != ENVIRON["S"]) next
      # ANCHORED to the end, where the stamper writes — the SAME grammar the already-identified
      # guard uses. A first-match (or any-position) read hands a claim the id its task merely
      # CITED in prose: two subjects wearing one id, and nothing prints ({#f3175968}). A [task]
      # line whose only token is a mid-prose citation therefore resolves to NOTHING here, and its
      # claim is left honestly BROKEN rather than fabricated-non-dangling. (Survey 2026-08-21:
      # 87 of 88 id-carrying [task] lines on this board are anchored; the one that is not is the
      # defect instance itself.)
      if (match(b, /\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$/)) last = substr(b, RSTART, RLENGTH) }
    END { if (last != "") print last }
  ' "$LOG" 2>/dev/null
}
auto_id(){  # <body> → body, with a {#id} appended when the line is a task or a LINKABLE claim
  local body="$1" id="" slug="" rest="" c1="" c2="" s=""
  [ "${MESH_CHAT_AUTOID:-1}" = 1 ] || { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }
  # ALREADY-IDENTIFIED guard. Its alphabet must be the SAME 8-hex grammar every READER uses
  # (_id_for_slug above), ANCHORED to the end of the body where the stamper writes — never "any
  # {#…} anywhere in prose". The wide form suppressed the stamp on exactly the best-cited rows:
  # a body that merely MENTIONED the syntax, or CITED a prior task's id (normal practice here),
  # disarmed its own stamp, and its later [taking]/[done] then inherited the CITED task's identity
  # via _id_for_slug — two subjects wearing one id, silently (witness, 2026-08-21, {#f3175968}).
  case "$body" in
    *'{#'[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]'}')
      printf '%s' "$body"; return 0 ;;                                # already identified — never re-stamp
  esac
  case "$body" in
    '[task] '*)
      slug="$(_slugcut "${body#\[task\] }")"
      [ -n "$slug" ] || { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }
      # An owner-first task computes the slug "owner" here; _id_for_slug refuses that key, so the
      # lookup finds nothing and the line MINTS. Mint-do-not-match is one rule, stated once, there.
      id="$(_id_for_slug "$slug")"
      [ -n "$id" ] || id="$(_mint_id)" || { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }
      ;;
    '[taking] '*|'[done] '*)
      rest="${body#\[taking\] }"; rest="${rest#\[done\] }"
      c1="$(_slugcut "$rest")"
      case "$rest" in *:*) c2="$(_trimsp "${rest#*:}")"; c2="${c2%% *}"; c2="${c2%:}" ;; esac
      for s in "$c1" "$c2"; do
        [ -n "$s" ] || continue
        id="$(_id_for_slug "$s")"
        [ -n "$id" ] && break
      done
      [ -n "$id" ] || { printf '%s' "$body"; return 0; }   # names no filed task → untouched; BROKEN is honest
      ;;
    *) printf '%s' "$body"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  printf '%s %s' "$body" "$id"
}

case "${1:-}" in
  --test)
    # Real dry-run of the load-bearing logic (was: only `[ -f LOG ] && [ -w LOG ]`, which passes even
    # if the multi-line→one-line collapse or the view's dated-line filter is broken — the two behaviours
    # that actually keep the board readable). Sandboxed in a temp MESH_DIR: NO write to the real board.
    # Existing tests below drive MESH_WHO directly to exercise dedupe/etc — self-derive would
    # clobber those synthetic identities if this pane happens to be TMUX-attached, so it's off
    # for the whole suite except the dedicated self-derive test (8.) which re-enables it locally.
    export MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=0
    # child invocations must re-enter THE FILE UNDER TEST, never whatever mesh-chat is first on
    # PATH — a bare $0 runs the DEPLOYED copy when the suite is invoked by name, so a genome edit
    # would be tested against the installed binary and pass while broken.
    _self="${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}"
    command -v grep >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no grep — view filter dead)"; exit 1; }
    command -v date >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no date)"; exit 1; }
    [ -f "$LOG" ] && [ -w "$LOG" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (real chat.log not writable)"; exit 1; }
    _td="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp)"; exit 1; }
    trap 'rm -rf "$_td"' EXIT
    # 1. a MULTI-LINE post must collapse to exactly ONE dated line (else continuations scatter the board)
    MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "$(printf 'alpha\nbeta\tgamma')" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (multi-line post didn't collapse to 1 line: got $_n)"; exit 1; }
    grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9T:-]+Z  .*::  alpha beta gamma$' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (post not dated/collapsed: $(cat "$_td/chat.log"))"; exit 1; }
    # 2. the view filter must KEEP the dated post and DROP a non-dated junk fragment
    printf 'JUNK fragment with no timestamp\n' >> "$_td/chat.log"
    _view="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" --tail 50)"
    printf '%s\n' "$_view" | grep -q 'alpha beta gamma' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (view dropped a valid dated line)"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s\n' "$_view" | grep -q 'JUNK fragment' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (view kept a non-dated junk line — filter broken)"; exit 1; }
    # 3. TARGETED-SEND validation: an unknown addressee must ERROR (exit 2) and post NOTHING (operator's
    #    one rule — fail fast, not silent-ignore). Use a definitely-bogus target so it fails regardless of
    #    which windows are live. Assert the sandbox log did NOT grow.
    _before=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" --to __nope__ "should not post" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _rc=$?
    [ "$_rc" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--to unknown target should exit 2, got $_rc)"; exit 1; }
    _after=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_before" = "$_after" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--to unknown target POSTED — must post nothing)"; exit 1; }
    # 4. HARDCAP (chat-review/idle-hardcap): an over-cap [idle] is TRIMMED to ≤cap on the board, STILL posts
    #    (trim-not-reject), full text tee'd to overflow; a short [idle] is untouched; a long [fyi] is NEVER capped.
    _before=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    # realistic word filler (NOT a repeated single char — that trips mesh-log-scrub's secret redaction);
    # 'ZTAIL' is a unique sentinel at the very END so it lands in overflow but is trimmed off the board.
    _fill="$(for i in $(seq 1 55); do printf 'w%02d ' "$i"; done)"   # 'w01 w02 … w55 ' ≈ 220 chars
    _long="[idle] ${_fill}ZTAIL"
    # DEDUPE=0 here: this probe is about the hardcap TRIM, not dedupe — and the over-cap + short idles below
    # are both zero-state green, which the structured-key dedupe (correctly) collapses; isolate so trimming
    # is what's measured (dedupe has its own tests 5/5b/5c).
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 "$0" "$_long" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _after=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_after" -gt "$_before" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (over-cap [idle] did not post — must TRIM, not reject)"; exit 1; }
    _bl="$(grep -a '  ::  \[idle\] w01' "$_td/chat.log" | tail -1)"; _bl="${_bl##*  ::  }"
    { [ -n "$_bl" ] && [ "${#_bl}" -le "${MESH_CHAT_IDLE_CAP_CHARS:-200}" ]; } || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (over-cap [idle] not trimmed to ≤cap on board: '${#_bl}')"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq 'ZTAIL' "$_td/chat-overflow.log" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (full [idle] text not preserved in overflow tier — sentinel missing)"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_bl" | grep -q 'ZTAIL' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (board line NOT trimmed — tail sentinel still present)"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'TRIMMED' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (over-cap [idle] gave no trim NOTE on stderr)"; exit 1; }
    _mid="all green nothing new this tick holding terse one liner"   # ≤ cap → must pass untouched
    MESH_DIR="$_td" MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 "$0" "[idle] $_mid" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq "\[idle\] $_mid\$" "$_td/chat.log" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (short [idle] wrongly trimmed)"; exit 1; }
    _fyi="$(for i in $(seq 1 55); do printf 'f%02d ' "$i"; done)FYEND"   # long [fyi] must pass untouched
    MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[fyi] $_fyi" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq 'FYEND' "$_td/chat.log" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (long [fyi] wrongly capped — cap must be [idle]-only)"; exit 1; }
    # 4b. VERIFY-MARKER LINT (chat-review/verify-marker-lint): a [verify] body opening with self-
    #     completion language ('X CONFIRMS ... REAL') must land on the board as [fyi], not raw
    #     [verify], with a NOTE on stderr; the SAME shape with 'CONFIRMED ... healthy' likewise; a
    #     genuine OPEN ask ('[verify] can someone check X?') must post UNCHANGED as [verify].
    _before=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] chat CONFIRMS the widget is healthy — REAL, not a guess" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _after=$(wc -l < "$_td/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_after" -gt "$_before" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-completed [verify] did not post — must relabel, not reject)"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[fyi\] chat CONFIRMS the widget is healthy' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-completed [verify] (CONFIRMS) not relabeled to [fyi]: $(tail -1 "$_td/chat.log"))"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] chat CONFIRMS' "$_td/chat.log" \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-completed [verify] (CONFIRMS) still landed under [verify])"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'relabeled to \[fyi\]' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-completed [verify] gave no relabel NOTE on stderr)"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] discover's find CONFIRMED healthy: matches artifact" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq '::  \[fyi\] .*CONFIRMED healthy' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-completed [verify] (CONFIRMED) not relabeled to [fyi])"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] can someone check the ds mic organ?" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] can someone check the ds mic organ?$' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (genuine open [verify] ask was wrongly relabeled)"; exit 1; }
    # falsifiability + toggle: MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 must let the same self-completed line through unrelabeled
    MESH_DIR="$_td" MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 "$0" "[verify] health CONFIRMS the toggle-off case — REAL" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] health CONFIRMS the toggle-off case' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 did not disable the relabel)"; exit 1; }
    # 4c. LEAD-ADDRESS LINT (chat-review/verify-leading-slug-addresses-nobody): a [verify] whose
    #     LEADING token names no live window books to mesh-promises' unpayable 'reflex-broadcast'
    #     dead-letter, and after the line exists only the poster can withdraw it. WARN-ONLY: the
    #     NOTE must appear on stderr while the body lands BYTE-UNCHANGED (never refused, never
    #     rewritten — a guessed debtor is worse than a dead-letter). Four arms + fail-open.
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] slug-naming-no-window-abc: OPEN claim for someone" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (slug-led [verify] gave no lead-address NOTE on stderr)"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] slug-naming-no-window-abc: OPEN claim for someone$' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (lead-address lint altered or dropped the body — it must be warn-only)"; exit 1; }
    # toggle: the identical line goes through UNLINTED under MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 (falsifiability)
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 "$0" "[verify] slug-naming-no-window-xyz: OPEN claim for someone" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 did not disable the lead-address lint)"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] slug-naming-no-window-xyz: OPEN claim for someone$' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (toggle-off lead-lint line did not land unchanged)"; exit 1; }
    # negative 1: a properly ADDRESSED [verify] (arrow form) must be silent — a false NOTE on a
    # correctly-routed claim is the harm to avoid, so this arm is what keeps the lint honest.
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] health -> genome: check the widget" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (lead-address lint false-NOTEd a correctly addressed [verify])"; exit 1; }
    # negative 2: a slug lead carrying an explicit `owner: <tool>/<window>` is already rescued
    # DOWNSTREAM by mesh-promises' owner-tag fallback → stay silent rather than duplicate it
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] slug-naming-no-window-own: owner: mesh-x/genome — check it" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (lead-address lint NOTEd a body carrying an explicit live owner: clause)"; exit 1; }
    # THE CLASS THIS LINT WAS BLIND TO (2026-08-24, board task
    # addressed-verify-dead-letters-because-the-arrow-grammar-is-unicode-only). The old test accepted
    # if ANY token of the 3-token address region resolved, so an ARROW form passed the moment its
    # FIRST token was a live window — no matter what followed the arrow, and no matter what
    # mesh-promises would actually do with the line. On the live board that covered 7 of the 8
    # arrow-addressed [verify]s, 2 of them standing LEAKED at 47-48h. Now the lint asks the reader
    # (`mesh-promises --address`), so an arrow pointing at a NON-window is NOTEd — the old code is
    # silent here, which is this leg's RED.
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] genome -> nosuchwindowxyz: check the widget" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (an arrow pointing at a NON-window gave no NOTE — the lint is still testing its own roster instead of asking the reader)"; exit 1; }
    # …and the SAME arrow pointing at a real window stays silent, in every separator form the board
    # writes (spaced/unspaced, ASCII/unicode/slash). This is the pair that proves the lint agrees with
    # the reader rather than merely being noisy: mesh-promises --address answers `genome` for all four.
    for _sep in '->' '→' '/'; do
      for _pad in '' ' '; do
        _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] health${_pad}${_sep}${_pad}genome: check the widget" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
        printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
          && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (lead-address lint false-NOTEd '[verify] health${_pad}${_sep}${_pad}genome: …' — a form the reader routes)"; exit 1; }
      done
    done
    # FAIL OPEN when the READER is unreachable: with mesh-promises off PATH the lint must ACCUSE
    # NOBODY (same rule as the valid_targets() fail-open — a blind read never accuses). This replaces
    # the old empty-tmux arm: the lint no longer reads tmux at all, so its blindness now lives here.
    # mesh-chat does not re-export PATH (:187), so stripping it really does hide the reader.
    _err="$(PATH=/usr/bin:/bin MESH_DIR="$_td" "$0" "[verify] slug-naming-no-window-blind: OPEN claim" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'names no live window' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (lead-address lint accused with the reader off PATH — must fail open)"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] slug-naming-no-window-blind: OPEN claim$' "$_td/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reader-absent path dropped or altered the body)"; exit 1; }
    # 4d. REPLY-LEG LINT (task a-terminal-baton-return-opens-a-claim-and-the-relabel-keys-on-the-
    #     wrong-word). A [verify] ANSWERING a claim the addressee already holds against you is a
    #     terminal report, not an ask: the ledger leaves your own claim standing AND bills them for
    #     work already done. The fixture is the live 08-22 pair, names changed to non-mind windows so
    #     the MESH_WHO self-derive (mind channels only) cannot rewrite the poster out from under it.
    #     mesh-promises is consulted through PATH, exactly as the lead lint is — so this leg also
    #     goes RED on a DEPLOY SKEW where mesh-chat has landed and its sibling reader has not.
    _rl="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="bravo@fixture"
    export MESH_PROMISE_ROSTER="alfa bravo charlie" MESH_PROMISE_BOARD_STORE=
    printf '2026-08-22T18:19:56Z  alfa@fixture  ::  [verify] alfa -> bravo: TWO open claims on the nvme per-sensor find, both falsifiable\n' > "$_rl/chat.log"
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_rl" "$0" "[verify] bravo -> alfa: BOTH your open claims ARE NOW SETTLED" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'REPLY LEG' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a [verify] answering an open claim gave no reply-leg NOTE — if mesh-promises on PATH has no --reply-leg, that is a DEPLOY SKEW, not a passing lint)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'two-open-claims-on-the-nvme-per-sensor-f' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reply-leg NOTE did not NAME the claim the line answers)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] bravo -> alfa: BOTH your open claims ARE NOW SETTLED$' "$_rl/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reply-leg lint altered, refused or dropped the body — it must be warn-only)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    # toggle (falsifiability) — the same line goes through unlinted under MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_rl" MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 "$0" "[verify] bravo -> alfa: BOTH your open claims ARE NOW SETTLED, toggle arm" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'REPLY LEG' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 did not disable the reply-leg lint)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    # NEGATIVE: a window that owes the addressee NOTHING must stay silent even when the prose is
    # nothing but settled words — this is the arm that proves the lint is not the phrase list again.
    export MESH_WHO="charlie@fixture"
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_rl" "$0" "[verify] charlie -> alfa: VERIFIED, DISCHARGED, CHECKED, SETTLED — every settled word there is" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'REPLY LEG' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reply-leg lint fired for a window owing the addressee nothing — it is reading PROSE)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    # FAIL OPEN when the READER is unreachable (same rule as the lead lint — a blind read never accuses)
    export MESH_WHO="bravo@fixture"
    _err="$(PATH=/usr/bin:/bin MESH_DIR="$_rl" "$0" "[verify] bravo -> alfa: reader-absent arm, both claims settled" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'REPLY LEG' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reply-leg lint accused with the reader off PATH — must fail open)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    grep -aq '::  \[verify\] bravo -> alfa: reader-absent arm, both claims settled$' "$_rl/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reader-absent path dropped or altered the body)"; rm -rf "$_rl"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_rl"; unset MESH_PROMISE_ROSTER MESH_PROMISE_BOARD_STORE

    # 5. IDLE DEDUPE: a same-state consecutive [idle] from the same mind is suppressed (not appended);
    #    a CHANGED [idle] posts; a repeated NON-[idle] marker is never deduped; MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 disables.
    _dd="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="ddtest@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_dd" "$0" "[idle] alpha state" >/dev/null 2>&1            # 1st idle → lands
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_dd" "$0" "[idle] alpha state" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"  # identical → suppressed
    _n=$(grep -c 'alpha state' "$_dd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identical [idle] not deduped: $_n copies)"; rm -rf "$_dd"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (dedupe did not NOTE on stderr)"; rm -rf "$_dd"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_dd" "$0" "[idle] beta state, 1 open task" >/dev/null 2>&1   # real state change (tasks 0→1) → lands
    grep -q 'beta state' "$_dd/chat.log" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (changed [idle] was wrongly suppressed)"; rm -rf "$_dd"; exit 1; }
    # 5b. SEMANTIC dedupe: an [idle] differing ONLY in volatile tokens (running 'N done' total, a timestamp,
    #     a commit hash) is the SAME state → suppressed; a real count change (claims/open-tasks) → posts.
    _sd="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="sigtest@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_sd" "$0" "[idle] board healthy (47 done, 0 claims), ambient STABLE 01:11Z" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sd" "$0" "[idle] board healthy (52 done, 0 claims), ambient STABLE 01:24Z" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _n=$(grep -c 'board healthy' "$_sd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reworded-but-unchanged [idle] not semantically deduped: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sd"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (semantic dedupe gave no NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_sd"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_sd" "$0" "[idle] board healthy (52 done, 2 claims), ambient STABLE 01:30Z" >/dev/null 2>&1   # claims 0→2 = real change
    _n=$(grep -c 'board healthy' "$_sd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (real claim-count change wrongly suppressed: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sd"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_sd"
    # 5c. STRUCTURED state-key (chat-review/idle-statekey-dedupe): two idles with WHOLLY DIFFERENT prose +
    #     cross-window narrative but the SAME state (0 tasks / 0 claims / green) collapse to ONE; a tier
    #     change (green→degraded) and a count change still post as a real new state.
    _sk="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="sktest@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_sk" "$0" "[idle] minds: green — tree clean, 0 open tasks, genome self-driving, all windows idle-green" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sk" "$0" "[idle] minds: converged, 0 tasks/claims, no new board activity since earlier, chat idle too" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_sk/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (reworded same-state idle not collapsed by structured key: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sk"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (structured-key dedupe gave no NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_sk"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_sk" "$0" "[idle] minds: DEGRADED — egress WARN, 0 tasks" >/dev/null 2>&1   # tier green→degraded = real change
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_sk/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (tier change green→degraded wrongly suppressed: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sk"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_sk" "$0" "[idle] minds: green again, 2 open tasks queued" >/dev/null 2>&1   # task-count 0→2 = real change
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_sk/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "3" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (open-task count change 0→2 wrongly suppressed: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sk"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_sk"
    # 5f. FINDING-window content-key (chat-review/idle-sig-discover-blind, 2026-07-22): discover posts
    #     DISTINCT dead-end findings as [idle] bodies (prose is the payload, no task/claim/tier token) —
    #     all collapsed to 0|0|green and got swallowed after the first. A finding window keys on a content
    #     fingerprint so two DIFFERENT findings BOTH land; an identical repeat still dedupes; and a
    #     NON-finding window (minds) with two different-prose 0|0|green idles STILL collapses (unchanged).
    _fw="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="discover@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_fw" "$0" "[idle] discover: mesh-sensorium x mesh-roz-channel — dead end, no fusion value" >/dev/null 2>&1
    MESH_DIR="$_fw" "$0" "[idle] discover: mesh-note3-say x mesh-tg-roz — dead end, redundant with the existing bridge" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] discover:' "$_fw/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (distinct discover findings collapsed to one key — the swallow bug: $_n, want 2)"; rm -rf "$_fw"; exit 1; }
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_fw" MESH_WHO="discover@host" "$0" "[idle] discover: mesh-sensorium x mesh-roz-channel — dead end, no fusion value" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"   # identical repeat → still deduped (same fingerprint)
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] discover:' "$_fw/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identical discover finding not deduped — content key too loose: $_n, want 2)"; rm -rf "$_fw"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identical discover finding gave no dedupe NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_fw"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_fw"
    # 5f-senses. SAME class for the SENSES window (chat-review/idle-dedupe-blind-senses, 2026-07-31): a
    #     perception reading names its state in domain words, not counts — two DIFFERENT observations that
    #     are both green collapsed to 0|0|green and the second was swallowed (verified live before the fix:
    #     "landed: 1", ×2 suppressed). Deliberately uses two GREEN bodies: a body carrying a degrade word in
    #     its leading clause ("tempo DEGRADED→BUSTLE") keys apart on tier ALONE and would pass RED-first
    #     without the fix — a vacuous gate. Identical repeat must still dedupe.
    _fs="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="senses@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_fs" "$0" "[idle] senses: room EMPTY->PRESENT, dwell 0h, ambient QUIET" >/dev/null 2>&1
    MESH_DIR="$_fs" "$0" "[idle] senses: household ACTIVE/HOME, climate STABLE, webcam clear" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] senses:' "$_fs/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (distinct senses observations collapsed to one key — the swallow bug: $_n, want 2)"; rm -rf "$_fs"; exit 1; }
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_fs" MESH_WHO="senses@host" "$0" "[idle] senses: room EMPTY->PRESENT, dwell 0h, ambient QUIET" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"   # identical repeat → still deduped
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] senses:' "$_fs/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identical senses idle not deduped — content key too loose: $_n, want 2)"; rm -rf "$_fs"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identical senses idle gave no dedupe NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_fs"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_fs"
    # 5f-control: the exemption is SCOPED — a non-finding window (minds) with two different-prose but
    # same-state 0|0|green idles must STILL collapse (not blanket-disabled by the finding-key change).
    _fc="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="minds@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_fc" "$0" "[idle] minds: mesh-sensorium x mesh-roz-channel — all green, nothing open" >/dev/null 2>&1
    MESH_DIR="$_fc" "$0" "[idle] minds: mesh-note3-say x mesh-tg-roz — all green, nothing open" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_fc/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (non-finding window over-distinguished on content — scope leak: $_n, want 1)"; rm -rf "$_fc"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_fc"
    # 5g. TASK-COUNT DRIFT BUCKET (chat-review/idle-sig-task-count-drift-defeats-dedupe, 2026-07-22): on a
    #     busy mesh the open-task count drifts ±1-2 EVERY cycle (79↔81) from background work completing during
    #     a sustained hold — the board's #1 repetitive line, because the absolute count minted a unique sig
    #     each time. Coarse-bucket (≥10 → nearest 10) dedupes that drift; a real shift (80→40) still posts;
    #     and low counts stay EXACT so 0→2 (idle→working) is NOT swallowed (that path is guarded by 5c above).
    _tc="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="tctest@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_tc" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 81 open tasks, dispatch held" >/dev/null 2>&1                 # lands
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_tc" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 80 open tasks, dispatch held" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"       # 81→80 drift → suppressed
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tc/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (±1 task-count drift 81→80 not deduped by bucket: $_n, want 1)"; rm -rf "$_tc"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'same-state \[idle\] suppressed' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (task-count drift bucket gave no dedupe NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_tc"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_tc" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 40 open tasks, dispatch held" >/dev/null 2>&1                 # 80→40 real shift → posts
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tc/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (real task-count shift 80→40 wrongly suppressed by bucket: $_n, want 2)"; rm -rf "$_tc"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_tc"
    # 5d. NARRATIVE degrade-word must NOT poison the tier: a green idle that merely NARRATES a past degrade
    #     in a hyphen-compound ('false-CRITICAL fix landed') is still tier=green and dedupes against a clean
    #     green idle — else any idle mentioning past work leaks. (The real leak observed live, 2026-06-25.)
    _sn="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="narr@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_sn" "$0" "[idle] minds: green — 0 tasks, all nominal" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sn" "$0" "[idle] minds: green — 0 tasks, the algedonic false-CRITICAL fix landed, board reconverged" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_sn/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (narrative degrade-word 'false-CRITICAL' poisoned tier → idle not deduped: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_sn"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_sn"
    # 5e. SPACE-LED trailing narrative degrade-word must NOT poison the tier either — the case 7b975c3's
    #     space-anchor MISSED (it only blocked hyphen-compounds): a standalone 'DEGRADED' in TRAILING prose
    #     ('… real DEGRADED→degraded …') is still tier=green because it's past the leading clause. Closed by
    #     leading-clause scope. (The recurrence chat [verify]'d live, 2026-06-25 13:16.)
    _se="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="trail@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_se" "$0" "[idle] chat — green, 0 tasks/claims" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_se" "$0" "[idle] chat — green, 0 tasks/claims; tier-extraction verify closed, re-verified from source (narrative green, real DEGRADED to degraded), loop done" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] chat' "$_se/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (SPACE-LED trailing 'DEGRADED' narrative poisoned tier → green idle not deduped: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_se"; exit 1; }
    # negative twin: a LEADING DEGRADED (real status) still tiers degraded and posts as a new state
    MESH_DIR="$_se" "$0" "[idle] chat — DEGRADED, egress WARN, 1 task" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] chat' "$_se/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (real LEADING-clause DEGRADED wrongly suppressed: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_se"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_se"
    # 5f. HEARTBEAT TTL (idle-heartbeat-ttl): an unchanged state-key re-emitted by the SAME who WITHIN the
    #     cadence window is suppressed EVEN when the immediately-preceding idle differed (oscillation /
    #     counter-reset). KEEPS the consecutive dedupe; ADDS a per-(who,sig) last-emit interval gate. A
    #     CHANGED sig emits immediately; the SAME sig AFTER the window emits again (a real heartbeat); TTL=0
    #     disables the interval gate.
    _tt="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="ttltest@host"
    # changed-sig-always-emits: A(tasks=0) then B(tasks=2, changed) → BOTH land
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 0 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 2 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tt/chat.log" 2>/dev/null; true); _n=${_n:-0}
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (changed-sig idle wrongly suppressed: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_tt"; exit 1; }
    # same-sig-within-TTL suppressed: post A again. Consecutive lets it through (last idle was B), but the TTL
    #     gate holds it (A emitted <window ago) — proving the gate goes BEYOND the consecutive check.
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_tt" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 0 open tasks, 0 claims" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tt/chat.log" 2>/dev/null; true); _n=${_n:-0}
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (same-sig within TTL not suppressed across an intervening idle: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_tt"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'heartbeat TTL' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (TTL suppression gave no heartbeat-TTL NOTE)"; rm -rf "$_tt"; exit 1; }
    # same-sig-after-TTL emits: a 1s window + 2s wait → the A heartbeat re-emits once its window elapsed.
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 5 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1   # C (changed) so A isn't the last idle
    sleep 2
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S=1 "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 0 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tt/chat.log" 2>/dev/null; true); _n=${_n:-0}
    [ "$_n" = "4" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (same-sig AFTER its TTL window did not re-emit: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_tt"; exit 1; }
    # TTL=0 disables the interval gate: a differing-last A (not consecutive) lands even within the window.
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 9 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1   # E (changed)
    MESH_DIR="$_tt" MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S=0 "$0" "[idle] minds: green, 0 open tasks, 0 claims" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c '  ::  \[idle\] minds:' "$_tt/chat.log" 2>/dev/null; true); _n=${_n:-0}
    [ "$_n" = "6" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_CHAT_IDLE_TTL_S=0 did not disable the interval gate: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_tt"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_tt"; export MESH_WHO="ddtest@host"
    MESH_DIR="$_dd" "$0" "[done] same result" >/dev/null 2>&1; MESH_DIR="$_dd" "$0" "[done] same result" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _n=$(grep -c 'same result' "$_dd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (non-[idle] marker wrongly deduped: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_dd"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_dd" MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 "$0" "[idle] beta state, 1 open task" >/dev/null 2>&1   # same state as prior; DEDUPE=0 → still lands
    _n=$(grep -c 'beta state' "$_dd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    [ "$_n" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 did not disable: $_n)"; rm -rf "$_dd"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_dd"
    # 6. HELP (chat-review/chat-help-leak): --help / -h must PRINT usage to stdout and POST NOTHING
    #    (the bug: '--help' fell to the *) catch-all and got posted to the board as a message).
    _hd="$(mktemp -d)"   # fresh sandbox: a no-op --help must never CREATE the board log
    _hout="$(MESH_DIR="$_hd" "$0" --help 2>/dev/null)"
    printf '%s' "$_hout" | grep -q 'mesh-chat <message>' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--help did not print usage)"; rm -rf "$_hd"; exit 1; }
    [ ! -s "$_hd/chat.log" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--help POSTED to the board — must post nothing)"; rm -rf "$_hd"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_hd" "$0" -h 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'mesh-chat <message>' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (-h alias did not print usage)"; rm -rf "$_hd"; exit 1; }
    [ ! -s "$_hd/chat.log" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (-h POSTED to the board — must post nothing)"; rm -rf "$_hd"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_hd"
    # 6b. INTERPRETER IS NOT AN IDENTITY (chat-review/a-python-tool-boards-as-the-interpreter-and-
    #     cannot-be-routed {#56cd2dbc}): a PYTHON-shebanged tool must board as ITS OWN SCRIPT NAME.
    #     Driven with a REAL python child calling this very script — never by unit-testing
    #     _caller_raw on a hand-built string, because the whole mechanism is /proc/$PPID/cmdline and
    #     a hand-built string is not a process. The env is cleared of MESH_WHO/TMUX so neither the
    #     explicit override nor the pane rescue can answer for the skip set under test.
    #     Falsifiable BY MUTATION: drop python3 from the :92 skip set → WHO is 'python3' → RED.
    _pi="$(mktemp -d)"
    cat > "$_pi/mesh-zz-pytool" <<'_PYEOT'
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import os, subprocess, sys
subprocess.run([sys.argv[1], "[fyi] interpreter-attribution probe"],
               env={**os.environ, "MESH_DIR": sys.argv[2], "MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE": "0"},
               stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
_PYEOT
    chmod +x "$_pi/mesh-zz-pytool"
    env -u MESH_WHO -u TMUX -u TMUX_PANE -u MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW "$_pi/mesh-zz-pytool" "$0" "$_pi" 2>/dev/null
    _piwho="$(sed -n 's/^[^ ]*  \([^ ]*\)  ::.*/\1/p' "$_pi/chat.log" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    [ -n "$_piwho" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (interpreter-attribution: the python child posted nothing, so the WHO assertion below would pass vacuously)"; rm -rf "$_pi"; exit 1; }
    case "$_piwho" in
      *python3*) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (interpreter-attribution: a python-shebanged tool boarded as '$_piwho' — the INTERPRETER, not its script. mesh-claim:163 reads the pre-@ half as a window to re-dispatch to, so a post like this can never be routed; 10 live board lines wore this, one of them an unrouted incident)"; rm -rf "$_pi"; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    case "$_piwho" in
      *mesh-zz-pytool*) : ;;
      *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (interpreter-attribution: WHO is '$_piwho', not the calling script's own name — it stopped naming the interpreter but still does not name the tool)"; rm -rf "$_pi"; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    rm -rf "$_pi"
    # 6c. …and when NOTHING can be resolved (a `python3 -c` one-liner outside any pane), the WHO must
    #     be a LOUD unattributed marker, never a name-shaped non-address. '<user>/python3@host' reads
    #     like an address and is not one — mesh-claim and scan_orphans take the pre-@ half as a window,
    #     find nothing, and route nothing SILENTLY. The cure lives at the call site, so the post has to
    #     say it is unattributed loudly enough for a human or a scan to find that call site.
    _pu="$(mktemp -d)"
    env -u MESH_WHO -u TMUX -u TMUX_PANE -u MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW MESH_DIR="$_pu" MESH_CHAT_DEDUPE=0 \
      python3 -c "import subprocess,sys; subprocess.run([sys.argv[1], '[fyi] unattributed one-liner probe'])" "$0" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _puwho="$(sed -n 's/^[^ ]*  \([^ ]*\)  ::.*/\1/p' "$_pu/chat.log" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    [ -n "$_puwho" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unattributed-marker: the -c one-liner posted nothing, so the assertion below would pass vacuously)"; rm -rf "$_pu"; exit 1; }
    case "$_puwho" in
      UNATTRIBUTED-*) : ;;
      *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unattributed-marker: an unresolvable caller boarded as '$_puwho' — a pre-@ half that looks like an address but routes nowhere; it must be visibly unattributed)"; rm -rf "$_pu"; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    rm -rf "$_pu"
    # 7. UNRECOGNIZED OPTION: --any-bogus-option must exit 2 and post NOTHING (chat-review/chat-option-fallback-post)
    _ud="$(mktemp -d)"
    MESH_DIR="$_ud" "$0" --bogus-option-here >/dev/null 2>&1
    _rc=$?
    [ "$_rc" = "2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unrecognized option should exit 2, got $_rc)"; rm -rf "$_ud"; exit 1; }
    [ ! -s "$_ud/chat.log" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unrecognized option posted to board — must post nothing)"; rm -rf "$_ud"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_ud"
    # 8. SELF-DERIVE (chat-review/chat-self-derive-who): a mismatched inherited MESH_WHO gets
    #    overridden to the pane's real window name + a stderr NOTE; a matching one is left alone
    #    and silent. MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW simulates the pane without needing real tmux.
    # [fyi] (never deduped/capped, unlike [idle]) so this test isolates self-derive from dedupe.
    _sw="$(mktemp -d)"
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sw" MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=1 MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW=pub MESH_WHO="genome@testhost" "$0" "[fyi] mismatch case" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    grep -aq '  pub@testhost  ::  \[fyi\] mismatch case$' "$_sw/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mismatched MESH_WHO not self-derived to pane window: $(cat "$_sw/chat.log")"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'MESH_WHO mismatch' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-derive override gave no NOTE on stderr)"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sw" MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=1 MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW=pub MESH_WHO="pub@testhost" "$0" "[fyi] match case" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    grep -aq '  pub@testhost  ::  \[fyi\] match case$' "$_sw/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (matching MESH_WHO wrongly altered)"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'MESH_WHO mismatch' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (matching MESH_WHO wrongly warned)"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    # non-mind-channel role identity (land@, doctor@, node-care@... — a per-call self-identify
    # prefix, not spawn drift) must survive UNTOUCHED even inside a mismatched pane, or this fix
    # would silently break the dozens of reflex tools that deliberately post under their own name.
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_sw" MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=1 MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW=pub MESH_WHO="land@testhost" "$0" "[fyi] role-identity case" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    grep -aq '  land@testhost  ::  \[fyi\] role-identity case$' "$_sw/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (non-mind-channel role identity wrongly self-derived away)"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'MESH_WHO mismatch' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (non-mind-channel role identity wrongly warned)"; rm -rf "$_sw"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_sw"
    # 8b. DERIVE-WHO must never build an identity out of the MESSAGE BODY (chat-review/
    #    chat-derive-who-eats-body 2026-07-14). Live signature it reproduces: a mind posting a
    #    [done] through a shell one-liner boarded as 'mesh-home/done___claim_id_of_596__@mesh-home'.
    #    Exercised through a REAL `bash -c` parent, so the assertion covers the actual /proc/PPID/
    #    cmdline shape that caused it — not a hand-fed string a refactor could drift away from.
    #    Asserts the EXACT expected WHO, not merely "no body words in it". A body-derived WHO fails
    #    this even when the payload happens to normalize into something that looks innocuous (the
    #    weak form of this assertion passed against the very bug it was written for) — that is this
    #    arm's real point and it is unchanged.
    #    THE EXPECTED STRING CHANGED 2026-08-30 ('<user>/bash@host' → 'UNATTRIBUTED-bash@host') with
    #    the interpreter-attribution fix above, and the reason applies to bash exactly as it did to
    #    python3: '<user>/bash' is not "honest", it only LOOKS like an address. mesh-claim:163 does
    #    win="${1%@*}" and scan_orphans reads the same half as a window, so both find nothing and
    #    route nothing, silently. The old wording called it honest because it correctly refused to
    #    invent a name; making it visibly unattributed refuses AND says so.
    #    TMUX/TMUX_PANE unset because --test itself runs INSIDE a mind's pane — leaving them set
    #    would make the "no pane" premise a lie and the fixture would assert the pane path instead.
    _dw="$(mktemp -d)"
    env -u TMUX -u TMUX_PANE bash -c 'MESH_DIR="$1" MESH_CHAT_SELFDERIVE=0 "$2" "[fyi] taking claim_id_of:596 body words" >/dev/null 2>&1' _ "$_dw" "$0"
    _who="$(sed -n '1s/  ::  .*//; 1s/^.*Z  //p' "$_dw/chat.log" 2>/dev/null)"
    [ "$_who" = "UNATTRIBUTED-bash@$HOST" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (script-less caller must board as 'UNATTRIBUTED-bash@$HOST', got '$_who' — a -c payload is data, not a caller)"; rm -rf "$_dw"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_dw"
    #    ...and in a pane, that same script-less caller must resolve to the pane's WINDOW — a real
    #    address, because mesh-claim:163 (win="${1%@*}") re-dispatches to the pre-@ half.
    _dw="$(mktemp -d)"
    bash -c 'MESH_DIR="$1" MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW=genome "$2" "[fyi] pane one-liner" >/dev/null 2>&1' _ "$_dw" "$0"
    grep -aq "  genome@$HOST  ::  \[fyi\] pane one-liner$" "$_dw/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (script-less caller in a pane must board as <window>@<host>, got: $(cat "$_dw/chat.log"))"; rm -rf "$_dw"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_dw"
    #    A caller that DOES name a script keeps its <user>/<script> tag — the fallback must not eat
    #    the caller-distinguishing identity every cron/reflex tool boards under.
    _dw="$(mktemp -d)"; printf '#!/bin/bash\nMESH_DIR="$1" MESH_CHAT_TEST_WINDOW=genome "$2" "[fyi] real script caller" >/dev/null 2>&1\n' > "$_dw/mesh-fake-reflex"
    chmod +x "$_dw/mesh-fake-reflex"; "$_dw/mesh-fake-reflex" "$_dw" "$0"
    grep -aq '  .*/mesh-fake-reflex@'"$HOST"'  ::  \[fyi\] real script caller$' "$_dw/chat.log" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a real script caller lost its <user>/<script> identity: $(cat "$_dw/chat.log"))"; rm -rf "$_dw"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_dw"
    # 9. TEST-SANDBOX gate (doctor-smoke-sandbox-net): under MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 a REAL-board post
    #    (default $HOME/.mesh) is swallowed with exit 0, while a REDIRECTED-MESH_DIR post (a test's
    #    own sandbox) still lands. HOME is remapped to a tmpdir so "real board" is itself a fixture.
    _gd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_gd/.mesh"
    HOME="$_gd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 "$0" "leak probe" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (sandbox-gated post must still exit 0)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
    [ ! -s "$_gd/.mesh/chat.log" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 post REACHED the real board: $(cat "$_gd/.mesh/chat.log"))"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
    HOME="$_gd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 "$0" --to genome "targeted leak probe" >/dev/null 2>&1
    [ ! -s "$_gd/.mesh/chat.log" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 --to post REACHED the real board)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
    HOME="$_gd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 MESH_DIR="$_gd/sandboxed" "$0" "sandboxed post" >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -aq 'sandboxed post' "$_gd/sandboxed/chat.log" 2>/dev/null \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (redirected-MESH_DIR post suppressed under sandbox env — self-sandboxed tests would lose their asserts)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_gd"
    # 10. chat-review/claim-release-on-done: a [done] naming a LIVE claim's key releases it (real
    #     mesh-claim binary, sandboxed via HOME only — MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 EXPLICITLY, because the
    #     doctor smoke-sweep exports MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 into every child --test and an inherited 1
    #     makes mesh-chat swallow the [claim]/[done] posts this fixture asserts on (claims ARE board
    #     lines) → deterministic FAIL under doctor while green standalone. HOME remap already makes
    #     leak-to-real-board impossible, so running unsandboxed here matches production safely;
    #     MESH_DIR left unset so it defaults to $HOME/.mesh inside the remapped HOME.
    if command -v mesh-claim >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      _cd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_cd/.mesh"
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim chat-review/claim-release-test-key >/dev/null 2>&1
      _v1="$(HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim --list 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1=="chat-review/claim-release-test-key"{print $4}')"
      [ "$_v1" = "LIVE" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (fixture claim not LIVE before [done]: '$_v1')"; rm -rf "$_cd"; exit 1; }
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 "$0" "[done] genome: chat-review/claim-release-test-key — finished, verified" >/dev/null 2>&1
      _v2="$(HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim --list 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1=="chat-review/claim-release-test-key"{print $4}')"
      [ -z "$_v2" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a [done] naming the claim key must release it, still: '$_v2')"; rm -rf "$_cd"; exit 1; }
      # negative: an UNRELATED claim must survive an unrelated [done] (no over-eager substring match)
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim chat-review/unrelated-claim-key >/dev/null 2>&1
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 "$0" "[done] genome: some other task entirely — finished" >/dev/null 2>&1
      _v3="$(HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim --list 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1=="chat-review/unrelated-claim-key"{print $4}')"
      [ "$_v3" = "LIVE" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unrelated claim wrongly released by an unrelated [done]: '$_v3')"; rm -rf "$_cd"; exit 1; }
      # chat-review/mesh-chat-donegate-collision: the claim owner and the [done] poster are
      # DIFFERENT identities (the real-world shape — 68a8132's ownership gate refuses a bare
      # `--done` here) — auto-release must still fire via --force. Pre-fix this claim stayed LIVE.
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 MESH_WHO="turbo-lid@host" mesh-claim chat-review/cross-owner-claim-key >/dev/null 2>&1
      HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 MESH_WHO="genome@host" "$0" "[done] genome: chat-review/cross-owner-claim-key — finished" >/dev/null 2>&1
      _v4="$(HOME="$_cd" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 mesh-claim --list 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1=="chat-review/cross-owner-claim-key"{print $4}')"
      [ -z "$_v4" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cross-owner [done] must still release the claim via --force, still: '$_v4')"; rm -rf "$_cd"; exit 1; }
      # EXPIRED IS NOT ABANDONED (mesh-claim task {#b2d997b4}): mesh-claim's --list column 4 gained a
      # fourth word for "past TTL but the owner is measurably LIVE and tending it". Every arm above
      # builds a FRESH claim, so all of them are LIVE and none can see a release path that tests
      # [ "$_verdict" = "LIVE" ] — the branch would silently stop releasing exactly the claims whose
      # owner posts [done] after their ttl lapsed, stranding a completion behind a lapsed clock.
      #
      # DRIVEN WITH A STUBBED mesh-claim, not a real expired claim. A real one needs an owner window
      # that mesh-mind-state reads LIVE, which makes the fixture a function of which windows happen to
      # exist on the node running the suite — measured: inside this harness the claim's WHO resolves to
      # the reflex identity rather than the pane's, and the row came back LIVE instead of EXPIRED. A
      # gate whose precondition depends on node furniture is a standing accusation waiting to happen
      # ([[a-suite-gate-on-a-node-fact-becomes-a-standing-accusation]]). What needs gating here is
      # mesh-chat's own SELECTION, so the stub states the column and this asserts what we do with it.
      # The stub sits in a fake HOME's .local/bin AND on PATH: mesh-chat itself does not re-export
      # PATH (:187), so a plain prepend is enough here — unlike inside mesh-claim, which does.
      _sd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_sd/.local/bin" "$_sd/.mesh"
      cat > "$_sd/.local/bin/mesh-claim" <<STUB
#!/bin/sh
case "\$1" in
  --list) printf 'stub/expired-key\tturbo-lid@host\t9000s\tEXPIRED\n'
          printf 'stub/stale-key\tturbo-lid@host\t9000s\tSTALE\n' ;;
  --done) printf '%s\n' "\$2" >> "$_sd/released" ;;
esac
exit 0
STUB
      chmod +x "$_sd/.local/bin/mesh-claim"
      HOME="$_sd" PATH="$_sd/.local/bin:$PATH" MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=0 \
        "$0" "[done] genome: stub/expired-key and stub/stale-key — finished" >/dev/null 2>&1
      grep -qx 'stub/expired-key' "$_sd/released" 2>/dev/null \
        || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a [done] naming an EXPIRED claim's key must still release it — an owner's own completion cannot be stranded by a lapsed clock; released: [$(cat "$_sd/released" 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')])"; rm -rf "$_cd" "$_sd"; exit 1; }
      # ...and STALE must still be left alone: that path belongs to mesh-claim-verify, which releases
      # only against [done] EVIDENCE. Without this arm the fix could be "release everything".
      grep -qx 'stub/stale-key' "$_sd/released" 2>/dev/null \
        && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a STALE claim was auto-released by a [done] — that path is mesh-claim-verify's, gated on evidence)"; rm -rf "$_cd" "$_sd"; exit 1; }
      rm -rf "$_sd"
      rm -rf "$_cd"
    fi
    # 11. WHO ROOT-DERIVE (chat-review/chat-who-root-derive): with MESH_WHO AND USER both unset (every
    #     cron reflex with no per-call prefix), the posted WHO must NEVER be '?@host' — it derives
    #     <login-user>/<caller>@host (ported from mesh-claim). Falsify: the old `${USER:-?}@host` minted
    #     '?@host' → the no-'?' assert FAILs. Subshell so the unset can't leak into later cells.
    _wd="$(mktemp -d)"
    ( unset MESH_WHO USER; MESH_DIR="$_wd" "$0" "[fyi] CRONWHO-sentinel" >/dev/null 2>&1 )
    _wl="$(grep -a 'CRONWHO-sentinel' "$_wd/chat.log" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
    _who="$(printf '%s' "$_wl" | sed -E 's/^[0-9][0-9T:Z-]*  //; s/  ::.*$//')"
    printf '%s' "$_who" | grep -q '?' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cron WHO minted an anonymous '?': '$_who')"; rm -rf "$_wd"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_who" | grep -qE '^[^ ]+/[^ ]+@[^ ]+$' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cron WHO not the <user>/<caller>@host caller-distinguishing shape: '$_who')"; rm -rf "$_wd"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_wd"
    # 12. BOARD LOCK (chat-review/board-append-merge-race): a post takes the shared board lock
    #     (~$MESH_DIR/.chat.lock — the one chat-sync's commit holds). A HELD lock delays the append
    #     (serialization) and the line still lands (wait, never drop). Falsify: an unlocked append
    #     returns instantly and would race the merge's snapshot→mv window.
    #     MEASURED IN MILLISECONDS, deliberately: `$(( SECONDS - _t0 )) -ge 1` counts second-BOUNDARY
    #     CROSSINGS, not elapsed time, so it asserts "a boundary fell inside the wait" — a coin flip
    #     weighted by where the tick landed, not a claim about flock. Its twin in mesh-chat-sync's
    #     race leg flaked ~1 run in 10 on a perfectly serializing lock and fronted autoland
    #     (2026-08-15); this is the same shape, fixed with it. Floor 600ms: the holder's `sleep 1` is
    #     a hard floor on the release, so a genuine wait cannot come in under ~900ms.
    #     What actually blocks is BOTH board-lock sites — hlc_stamp's fd 8 (`-w 2`) reaches the held
    #     lock first and absorbs most of the wait, append_log's fd 9 (`-w 5`) takes it after; the
    #     mutant that turns this leg red has to make BOTH non-blocking (making only fd 9 `-n` still
    #     waits ~925ms and passes), so read the leg as "a post serializes", not "append_log does".
    _now_ms(){ # ms since epoch; EPOCHREALTIME's radix char is LOCALE-dependent (. or ,)
      local _r="${EPOCHREALTIME:-}"
      if [ -n "$_r" ]; then _r="${_r//[.,]/}"; printf '%s' "$(( 10#$_r / 1000 ))"
      else date +%s%3N; fi
    }
    _ld="$(mktemp -d)"
    MESH_DIR="$_ld" "$0" "[fyi] LOCK-sentinel-free" >/dev/null 2>&1
    [ -e "$_ld/.chat.lock" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (append did not create/take the board lockfile)"; rm -rf "$_ld"; exit 1; }
    flock "$_ld/.chat.lock" -c 'sleep 1' & _lh=$!
    sleep 0.1
    _lt0=$(_now_ms)
    MESH_DIR="$_ld" "$0" "[fyi] LOCK-sentinel-held" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _lel=$(( $(_now_ms) - _lt0 )); wait "$_lh" 2>/dev/null
    grep -aq 'LOCK-sentinel-held' "$_ld/chat.log" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (append under a held lock never landed — lock must delay, not drop)"; rm -rf "$_ld"; exit 1; }
    [ "$_lel" -ge 600 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (post did not wait on the held board lock (elapsed ${_lel}ms, want >=600) — flock not serializing at hlc_stamp fd8 / append_log fd9)"; rm -rf "$_ld"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_ld"
    # 13. HLC CAUSAL STAMP (chat-review/hlc-causal-order, 2026-07-23 — Kulkarni/Demirbas 2014 via the
    #     08:50 lit-review). The stamp IS the board's sort key (chat-sync converges by LC_ALL=C sort),
    #     and two same-wall-second posts used to stamp IDENTICALLY — so their converged order was
    #     whatever WHO/body happened to sort to, and a [taking] could sort BEFORE the [task] it answers
    #     (the phantom-strand race). The HLC send rule (l := max(l_prev+1, pt), counter folded into the
    #     seconds field) makes every post's stamp STRICTLY greater than this node's previous post and
    #     than any peer stamp adopted by chat-sync's receive-merge — while the line format stays
    #     byte-identical, so NO timestamp-parsing reader needs sweeping.
    _hc="$(mktemp -d)"; export MESH_WHO="hlctest@host"
    _s0=0; _s1=1
    for _try in 1 2 3 4 5; do
      rm -f "$_hc/chat.log" "$_hc/hlc"
      _s0=$(date +%s)
      MESH_DIR="$_hc" "$0" "[fyi] hlc-A first" >/dev/null 2>&1
      MESH_DIR="$_hc" "$0" "[fyi] hlc-B second" >/dev/null 2>&1
      _s1=$(date +%s)
      [ "$_s0" = "$_s1" ] && break
    done
    [ "$_s0" = "$_s1" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: 5 tries couldn't land two posts inside one wall second)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    _tA="$(grep -a 'hlc-A first' "$_hc/chat.log" | awk '{print $1}')"
    _tB="$(grep -a 'hlc-B second' "$_hc/chat.log" | awk '{print $1}')"
    { [ -n "$_tA" ] && [ -n "$_tB" ]; } || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: same-second posts missing from board)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    [ "$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$_tA" "$_tB" | LC_ALL=C sort -u | wc -l)" = 2 ] && [ "$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$_tA" "$_tB" | LC_ALL=C sort | head -1)" = "$_tA" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: same-second posts not STRICTLY increasing (A='$_tA' B='$_tB') — causal order lost at the converge sort)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    # format control: the bumped stamp keeps the plain second-granularity shape EVERY reader parses —
    # this is the every-reader-sweep contract (a-format-fix-must-sweep-every-reader) held by design.
    printf '%s' "$_tB" | grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z$' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: stamp format changed ('$_tB') — the byte-stable-format contract is broken)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    # clock-regression / receive-merge monotonicity: with state AHEAD of wall (a peer stamp adopted by
    # chat-sync, or a backwards wall-clock jump), the next post must stamp state+1 — never the older wall.
    _seed=$(( $(date +%s) + 5 ))
    rm -f "$_hc/chat.log"; printf '%s' "$_seed" > "$_hc/hlc"
    MESH_DIR="$_hc" "$0" "[fyi] hlc-C ahead" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _tC="$(grep -a 'hlc-C ahead' "$_hc/chat.log" | awk '{print $1}')"
    _eC="$(date -d "$_tC" +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    [ "$_eC" = "$(( _seed + 1 ))" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: state-ahead post stamped epoch $_eC, want seed+1=$(( _seed + 1 )) — monotonicity vs merged peer stamps lost)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    # drift cap: a corrupt far-future state (would poison every later stamp AND trip chat-sync's
    # future-repair into rewriting real posts to yesterday) is clamped to wall+cap with a LOUD note.
    rm -f "$_hc/chat.log"; printf '%s' "$(( $(date +%s) + 500 ))" > "$_hc/hlc"
    _err="$(MESH_DIR="$_hc" "$0" "[fyi] hlc-D corrupt" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    _tD="$(grep -a 'hlc-D corrupt' "$_hc/chat.log" | awk '{print $1}')"
    _eD="$(date -d "$_tD" +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    { [ "$_eD" -gt 0 ] && [ "$_eD" -le "$(( $(date +%s) + 91 ))" ]; } || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: corrupt +500s state adopted (epoch $_eD) — drift cap dead)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$_err" | grep -q 'drift' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (hlc: drift clamp fired silently — must NOTE on stderr)"; rm -rf "$_hc"; exit 1; }
    unset MESH_WHO; rm -rf "$_hc"

    # ── ARCHIVE READ PATH (board-archive-unreachable, 2026-08-20) ───────────────────────────────
    # Drives --history against a SANDBOX store: a --test that ingested into the real
    # ~/.mesh/board-store.db would be writing the durable record it exists to check.
    # The load-bearing assertion is that the answer comes from the STORE and not the board — the
    # sentinel is seeded into the archive ONLY and must be absent from the sandbox chat.log, so a
    # --history that secretly read $LOG cannot pass.
    _ha="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp)"; exit 1; }
    _hreal="$MESH_DIR/board-store.db"; _hrealsz="$(wc -c < "$_hreal" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)"
    # (a) archive ABSENT is a LOUD n/a (exit 2) — never an empty-stdout success that reads as "the
    #     board never said that".
    _hs_out="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/nope.db" "$_self" --history 2>/dev/null)"; _hs_rc=$?
    [ "$_hs_rc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: missing archive exited $_hs_rc, want 2 — a dead archive must be n/a, not empty)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
    [ -z "$_hs_out" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: missing archive printed rows)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
    if command -v mesh-docstore >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      printf '%s\n' \
        '2026-08-19T13:24:49Z  tg-roz@x  ::  [done] ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL' \
        '2026-08-19T15:01:20Z  tg-roz@x  ::  [fyi] second archived line DISCRIMINAND' \
        'undated junk must not be returned JUNKTOKEN' \
        | mesh-docstore --file "$_ha/arch.db" ingest >/dev/null 2>&1
      grep -aq 'ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL' "$_ha/chat.log" 2>/dev/null && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: sentinel leaked into the sandbox board — the test cannot prove the store was the source)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # (b) an EVICTED line (in the store, absent from the board) is returned
      _hb="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/arch.db" "$_self" --history ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL 2>/dev/null)"; _hb_rc=$?
      [ "$_hb_rc" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: archived line lookup exited $_hb_rc, want 0)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_hb" | grep -aq 'ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: a line present ONLY in the archive was not returned — the eviction-proof tier is unreachable)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # whole-store query ('' = no pattern): the dated filter is the ONLY thing dropping the junk
      # here, so a removed filter shows. Asserting it through a pattern grep proved nothing — the
      # junk line did not match the sentinel either way (mutant survived, 2026-08-20).
      _hj="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/arch.db" "$_self" --history '' 10 2>/dev/null)"
      printf '%s' "$_hj" | grep -aq 'ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: whole-store query lost the archived line)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_hj" | grep -aq 'undated junk' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: undated junk returned — dated-line filter dead)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # …and the SAME filter on the PATTERN branch, which the whole-store leg above cannot reach:
      # a pattern that hits ONLY the undated line must come back empty (exit 1), not return it.
      # Without this the pattern branch's filter was unasserted and a mutant removing it survived
      # (2026-08-20) — the junk simply never matched the sentinel being searched for.
      _hjp="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/arch.db" "$_self" --history JUNKTOKEN 2>/dev/null)"; _hjp_rc=$?
      [ "$_hjp_rc" = 1 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: pattern hitting only an UNDATED line exited $_hjp_rc, want 1 — dated filter dead on the pattern branch)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      [ -z "$_hjp" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: undated line returned by a pattern query)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # (c) a pattern that MISSES is a DIFFERENT fact from a dead archive: exit 1, empty stdout, and
      #     the live doc count on stderr so the two are never confused at a glance. The count is 3,
      #     not 2: the STORE holds the undated junk (mesh-chat-sync's board_archive greps before it
      #     ingests; the store itself filters nothing), and --history drops it at READ — which is
      #     exactly what leg (b) asserts.
      _hc_err="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/arch.db" "$_self" --history ZZZ-NO-SUCH-LINE-ZZZ 2>&1 >/dev/null)"; _hc_rc=$?
      [ "$_hc_rc" = 1 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: no-match exited $_hc_rc, want 1 — must differ from the n/a 2)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_hc_err" | grep -q 'archive LIVE: 3 docs' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: no-match did not report the live doc count — 'archive dead' and 'pattern missed' read identically)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # (d) a bare-DIGIT pattern is searched, never silently taken as N (msg ids/timestamps are the
      #     commonest archive queries)
      _hd="$(MESH_DIR="$_ha" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_ha/arch.db" "$_self" --history 13:24:49 2>/dev/null)"
      printf '%s' "$_hd" | grep -aq 'ARCHIVE-ONLY-SENTINEL' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: numeric pattern was not searched as a pattern)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # …and it must EXCLUDE the other dated line. Without this, an implementation that silently
      # took the digits as N would return everything — including the sentinel — and pass (mutant
      # survived, 2026-08-20): co-occurrence is not selection.
      printf '%s' "$_hd" | grep -aq 'DISCRIMINAND' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history: numeric arg taken as a COUNT, not a pattern — non-matching line returned)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
      # (e) the REAL durable store is untouched by the whole leg
      _hnow="$(wc -c < "$_hreal" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)"
      [ "$_hnow" = "$_hrealsz" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--history test wrote the REAL board archive: $_hrealsz -> $_hnow bytes)"; rm -rf "$_ha"; exit 1; }
    else
      echo "NOTE: --history archive legs SKIPPED (mesh-docstore absent) — only the n/a path was asserted." >&2
    fi
    rm -rf "$_ha"
    # 14. AUTO-ID — the board's links minted by the writer. Behaviour, never source text: post through
    #     THE FILE UNDER TEST into a sandbox board, then read the verdicts of the REAL reader
    #     (the genome's mesh-board-id BESIDE this script, never whatever is first on PATH — a bare
    #     name would audit the deployed copy while a genome edit sails through).
    _ai="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp autoid)"; exit 1; }
    _bid="$(dirname "$_self")/mesh-board-id"
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/auto-id-demo: first filing' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _t1="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/auto-id-demo' "$_ai/chat.log" | tail -1)"
    printf '%s' "$_t1" | grep -qE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: a [task] posted with NO {#id} token — adoption is back to 0: $_t1)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    _id="$(printf '%s' "$_t1" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}' | head -1)"
    # the LINK: a claim naming that task carries the SAME token
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[taking] genome: tool-x/auto-id-demo — starting' >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -a '\[taking\] genome: tool-x/auto-id-demo' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -qF "$_id" \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: the claim did not carry its task's token $_id — the claim is linked by slug text only, which is the pre-fix state)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # a re-WORD under the same slug keeps ONE identity (a second mint would fork the task in two)
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/auto-id-demo: reworded, same slug, more detail' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _nid="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/auto-id-demo' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}' | sort -u | wc -l)"
    [ "$_nid" = 1 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: one re-worded task now carries $_nid distinct ids — identity is not stable across a re-file)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # a claim naming NO filed task gets NOTHING: fabricating a token turns BROKEN into DANGLING, worse
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[taking] genome: tool-x/never-filed-slug — orphan claim' >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -a 'never-filed-slug' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -qE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: minted a token for a claim naming no [task] — a DANGLING id reads worse than an honest BROKEN)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # the off-switch leaves the body byte-identical
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" MESH_CHAT_AUTOID=0 "$_self" '[task] tool-x/off-switch: no token wanted' >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -a 'tool-x/off-switch' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -q '{#' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: MESH_CHAT_AUTOID=0 still stamped a token)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # a non-claim line is never stamped (the board is mostly [fyi]/[idle]/sensor marks — this must not
    # scatter tokens across lines no auditor reads)
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[fyi] tool-x/auto-id-demo: an ordinary note naming the same slug' >/dev/null 2>&1
    grep -a '\[fyi\] tool-x/auto-id-demo' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -q '{#' \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: stamped a [fyi] — alphabet must match the reader's)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # A CITATION IS NOT A STAMP (2026-08-21, {#f3175968}): the already-identified guard's alphabet used
    # to be ANY {#…} anywhere in prose, while every reader's grammar is the anchored 8-hex form. So a
    # body that merely mentioned the syntax, or cited a prior task's id — normal, encouraged practice
    # here — silently suppressed its OWN stamp, and its later claims then resolved by slug to the CITED
    # task's identity: two subjects, one id, and nothing printed. Both directions asserted.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/cite-mid-prose: this re-files after {#9d6d48b0} and mentions {#…} too' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _ct="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/cite-mid-prose' "$_ai/chat.log" | tail -1)"
    printf '%s' "$_ct" | grep -qE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$' \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: a [task] body CITING another id went unstamped — a mid-prose citation must be a citation, not a stamp: $_ct)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    _cid="$(printf '%s' "$_ct" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ "$_cid" = '{#9d6d48b0}' ] \
      && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: the task wears the id it CITED instead of its own — identity donated: $_ct)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # THE OTHER HALF OF THE DONATION: the claim. beta's slug must resolve to BETA's own token, never
    # to the alpha token beta's body merely cites — _id_for_slug reads the anchored form for the same
    # reason the guard does.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/alpha-one: first subject' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _aid="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/alpha-one' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" "[task] tool-x/beta-two: unrelated subject, cites $_aid mid-prose" >/dev/null 2>&1
    _bid2="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/beta-two' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[taking] genome: tool-x/beta-two' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _cid2="$(grep -a '\[taking\] genome: tool-x/beta-two' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ -n "$_bid2" ] && [ "$_cid2" = "$_bid2" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: a claim on beta wears '$_cid2', not beta's own '$_bid2' (alpha is '$_aid') — a cited id was donated to another subject's claims)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # and the anchored guard still refuses to double-stamp an already-identified body
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/already-stamped: filed with its token {#abcdef12}' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _st="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/already-stamped' "$_ai/chat.log" | tail -1)"
    [ "$(printf '%s' "$_st" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}' | wc -l)" = 1 ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: an already-identified body was stamped a SECOND time: $_st)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # THE CONTRACT BELONGS TO THE READER, so assert it WITH the reader. A token this writer mints that
    # mesh-board-id cannot parse, or that leaves its counters at zero, heals exactly nothing — which is
    # the whole failure this change exists to end.
    if [ -x "$_bid" ]; then
      _bout="$(MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_bid" check 2>/dev/null)"
      printf '%s' "$_bout" | grep -qE '\([1-9][0-9]* id-tracked\)' \
        || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: mesh-board-id still counts 0 id-tracked after auto-stamping — writer/reader token contract broken): $_bout"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_bout" | grep -q "REFILED  $_id" \
        || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: the reader sees no REFILED lineage for the re-worded task — the carried token is not the same token): $_bout"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_bout" | grep -q 'BROKEN .*never-filed-slug' \
        || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id: the genuinely unlinkable claim LOST its BROKEN verdict — auto-id must not paper an orphan over): $_bout"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    else
      echo "NOTE: auto-id reader-contract legs SKIPPED (no mesh-board-id beside $_self) — only the writer side was asserted." >&2
    fi
    # THE ARROW-OWNER FORM ({#4053729b}). The board files tasks as "<slug> → owner: <tool>/<window>",
    # so the arrow lands BEFORE the first colon: a colon-only cut read the TASK slug as "<slug> → owner"
    # while its claim read "<slug>", and the two could never compare equal. The token was minted fine
    # and the CARRY never fired — 35 of 136 live [task] lines wore that form, every claim on them
    # structurally unlinkable. Seen RED on the pre-fix code: the claim below landed with no token.
    #
    # ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING HERE: the sibling task is filed BEFORE the claim, so it is the NEWER
    # candidate. A cut that is right but a COMPARISON that is loose (prefix instead of equality)
    # would then hand the claim on "arrow-form" the id of "arrow-form-two" — the same donation harm
    # {#f3175968} closed, reached through the matcher instead of the cut. Claim-after-sibling is the
    # only ordering in which that mutant is visible.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/arrow-form → owner: tool-x/genome. body text after' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _arid="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/arrow-form → owner: tool-x/genome. body text after' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    # NEGATIVE 1 — the cut must not FOLD two subjects into one identity. Two slugs sharing a prefix
    # stay two ids, and each claim routes to its own.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/arrow-form-two → owner: tool-x/genome. a DIFFERENT subject' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _ar2="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/arrow-form-two →' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ -n "$_arid" ] && [ -n "$_ar2" ] && [ "$_ar2" != "$_arid" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id arrow-form NEGATIVE: 'arrow-form-two' ($_ar2) collapsed onto 'arrow-form' identity ($_arid) — two subjects, one id)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # POSITIVE: the claim on the SHORTER slug carries ITS task's id, not the newer sibling's.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[taking] genome: tool-x/arrow-form' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _arc="$(grep -a '\[taking\] genome: tool-x/arrow-form' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ "$_arc" = "$_arid" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id arrow-form: a claim on an arrow-owner task wears '$_arc', not its task's '$_arid' (the sibling is '$_ar2') — writer and reader disagree on where the slug ends, or the match is not an equality)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[done] tool-x/arrow-form-two: finished' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _arc2="$(grep -a '\[done\] tool-x/arrow-form-two' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ "$_arc2" = "$_ar2" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id arrow-form NEGATIVE: the claim on arrow-form-two wears '$_arc2', want its own '$_ar2')"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # THE WRITER SIDE carries its own leg: a RE-FILE of the same arrow task must find its OWN prior
    # id, not mint a second one. The reader-side cut alone links claims but leaves the writer
    # computing "<slug> → owner" as its lookup key, so the re-file mints a fresh identity and one
    # subject splits in two — the exact failure ids exist to prevent, moved one line over.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] tool-x/arrow-form → owner: tool-x/genome. RE-FILED, reworded body' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _arre="$(grep -a '\[task\] tool-x/arrow-form → owner: tool-x/genome. RE-FILED' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ "$_arre" = "$_arid" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id arrow-form: a RE-FILE of the arrow task minted '$_arre' instead of carrying its own '$_arid' — one subject split into two identities)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # NEGATIVE 2 — a PROSE arrow is not an owner clause. The live board carries
    # "[task] operator sent document X.docx -> /home/…"; cutting at a BARE arrow would read both of
    # these as one slug and donate a single identity to both. Only "→/-> owner" cuts.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] operator sent a file -> /home/x/inbox/a.docx' >/dev/null 2>&1
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] operator sent a file -> /home/x/inbox/b.docx' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _pa="$(grep -a 'operator sent a file -> /home/x/inbox/a.docx' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    _pb="$(grep -a 'operator sent a file -> /home/x/inbox/b.docx' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ -n "$_pa" ] && [ -n "$_pb" ] && [ "$_pa" != "$_pb" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id arrow-form NEGATIVE: two prose lines differing only AFTER a non-owner arrow share identity '$_pa'/'$_pb' — the arrow cut is too broad)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # THE OWNER-FIRST FORM ({#bdb24088}) — the MIRROR of the arrow form above, and the one CLAUDE.md
    # actually prescribes ("[task] owner: <tool>/<window> — <subject>"; dispatch routes on it). The
    # colon-only cut yields the literal slug "owner" for EVERY such task, so they all collapsed onto
    # ONE identity: two subjects 2.5h apart both wore {#670a87a5}, a thread already [done], and the
    # second was born DISCHARGED — never dispatchable, never ageing as an open promise. Seen RED on
    # the pre-fix code: O1 below returned two identical ids.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] owner: tool-x/genome -- FIRST owner-first subject. slug: owner-first-alpha' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _o1="$(grep -a 'FIRST owner-first subject' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] owner: tool-x/senses -- SECOND, entirely unrelated subject. slug: owner-first-beta' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _o2="$(grep -a 'SECOND, entirely unrelated subject' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ -n "$_o1" ] && [ -n "$_o2" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id owner-first: an owner-first [task] went UNSTAMPED ('$_o1'/'$_o2') — mint-do-not-match must still MINT, an untokened task is invisible to the reader)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    [ "$_o1" != "$_o2" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id owner-first: two unrelated owner-first tasks both wear '$_o1' — every owner-first task collapsed onto the literal slug 'owner', and the second is born inside the first's thread)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # NEGATIVE — the guard is the CUT'S OWN OUTPUT, not a "^owner" prefix guess. A real subject slug
    # that merely STARTS with the letters "owner" keeps its colon cut and stays matchable, so a
    # re-file of it carries its own id instead of splitting one subject into two identities.
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[task] ownership-audit: who owns the retired units' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _ow1="$(grep -a '\[task\] ownership-audit: who owns' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    # EACH HALF CARRIES ITS OWN DIRECT ARM, because end-to-end they MASK each other: with the
    # reader half in place, deleting the writer half still produces two distinct ids (the lookup
    # simply finds nothing and mints), so an e2e-only suite calls a one-line mutant GREEN. Measured
    # on this very change. So the grammar is pinned where it is DECIDED, at both sites.
    # THE PREMISE, asserted rather than assumed: the shared cut really does yield "owner" for the
    # form CLAUDE.md prescribes. If that ever stops being true the guard below is guarding nothing,
    # and it would go on passing in silence.
    [ "$(_slugcut 'owner: tool-x/genome -- a subject')" = "owner" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id owner-first: the shared cut no longer yields 'owner' for the prescribed task form — the premise the guard rests on moved, re-derive it)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # THE RULE, driven directly at the one site that carries it: with an owner-first [task] on the
    # board, the key "owner" must resolve to NOTHING. A match here IS the defect — the next
    # owner-first task inherits that thread. (The e2e arms above cannot see a writer-side mutant,
    # which is exactly why the rule lives here and is asserted here.)
    [ -z "$(LOG="$_ai/chat.log" _id_for_slug owner)" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id owner-first READER half: the key 'owner' resolved to an existing task ($(LOG="$_ai/chat.log" _id_for_slug owner)) — every owner-first task collapses onto that thread)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    MESH_DIR="$_ai" "$_self" '[taking] genome: ownership-audit' >/dev/null 2>&1
    _ow2="$(grep -a '\[taking\] genome: ownership-audit' "$_ai/chat.log" | grep -oE '\{#[0-9a-f]{8}\}$')"
    [ -n "$_ow1" ] && [ "$_ow1" = "$_ow2" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id owner-first NEGATIVE: a claim on the real slug 'ownership-audit' wears '$_ow2', want its task's '$_ow1' — the owner guard over-reached from an exact match to a prefix)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    # CANDIDATE-PREFIX guard ({#6d58993b}). The candidate test and the cut are now ONE string
    # (MARK, in _id_for_slug), so they cannot drift; this arm asserts the RULE that string encodes,
    # at any offset a future edit might choose. The reported mutant widened the candidate test from
    # the 7-char task marker to a bare 2-char prefix and the WHOLE suite stayed green: the admitted
    # [taking]/[done] lines were merely MIS-cut by two characters, and nothing anywhere compared the
    # offset against the marker. So: a line that is not a [task] is NEVER a source of a task id.
    # The two claim lines below are the ONLY carriers of {#deadbee1} in this log and no [task] named
    # phantom-slug was ever filed, so every key a widened marker could cut out of them — at the old
    # typed offset 8 and at a derived one alike — must resolve to NOTHING.
    # THE HARM if one did: a [task] later filed under that slug is born wearing the id of a thread
    # already [done] — born DISCHARGED, never dispatchable, never ageing as an open promise. That is
    # {#670a87a5} reached through the candidate test instead of through the cut, and its failure
    # direction is the same SILENCE.
    printf '%s  genome@t  ::  [taking] genome: phantom-slug {#deadbee1}\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" >> "$_ai/chat.log"
    printf '%s  genome@t  ::  [done] phantom-slug: closed by hand {#deadbee1}\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" >> "$_ai/chat.log"
    for _ck in 'phantom-slug' '] genome' 'done] phantom-slug' 'taking] genome'; do
      _cv="$(LOG="$_ai/chat.log" _id_for_slug "$_ck")"
      [ -z "$_cv" ] \
        || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id candidate-prefix: the key '$_ck' resolved to $_cv, an id that lives ONLY on claim lines — a non-[task] line became a source of task ids, so the next task filed under that slug is born inside a settled thread)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    done
    # AND the guard must not have eaten the real thing: a genuine [task] still resolves.
    [ -n "$(LOG="$_ai/chat.log" _id_for_slug ownership-audit)" ] \
      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (auto-id candidate-prefix NEGATIVE: a real [task] slug 'ownership-audit' now resolves to NOTHING — the candidate test or the derived offset excludes the task form itself)"; rm -rf "$_ai"; exit 1; }
    rm -rf "$_ai"
    # ── OMISSION DETECTION (--gaps): the board's COMPLETENESS inside its own window ──────────────
    # Every leg was seen RED before it was seen green (mutants: dropping the ceiling bound made G4
    # report the later arrivals as holes; dropping the by-body subtraction made G7 report a rewrite
    # as a loss; making the missing-archive arm print "COMPLETE" and exit 0 made G5 pass a board
    # nothing had corroborated — the exact honest-fusion failure the arm exists to prevent).
    if command -v mesh-docstore >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      _gd="$(mktemp -d)"; _gstore="$_gd/store.db"
      # a 3-line board and an archive that saw the same 3 lines: COMPLETE
      printf '%s\n' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:01Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] one' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:02Z  b@n  ::  [fyi] two' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:03Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] three' > "$_gd/chat.log"
      mesh-docstore --file "$_gstore" ingest < "$_gd/chat.log" >/dev/null 2>&1
      _gout="$(MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps 2>/dev/null)"; _grc=$?
      [ "$_grc" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G1: a board carrying every archived in-window line exited $_grc, want 0)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_gout" | grep -q 'COMPLETE' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G1: did not report COMPLETE — got: $_gout)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G2 THE HOLE: the middle line vanishes from the board, the archive still has it → 1 omission,
      # NAMED. This is the 2026-08-19 shape reduced to three lines.
      grep -v 'two' "$_gd/chat.log" > "$_gd/x" && mv "$_gd/x" "$_gd/chat.log"
      _gout="$(MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps 2>/dev/null)"; _grc=$?
      [ "$_grc" = 1 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G2: an in-window archived line missing from the board exited $_grc, want 1)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_gout" | grep -q '1 OMISSION' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G2: wrong count — got: $_gout)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_gout" | grep -q 'fyi\] two' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G2: the omission was counted but never NAMED — a count with no line is unrecoverable)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G3 BELOW THE FLOOR IS THE DESIGN, NOT A LOSS: an archived line older than the board's oldest
      # is exactly what `tail -N` is FOR. Counting it would make every capped board permanently red.
      printf '%s\n' '2026-07-01T00:00:00Z  c@n  ::  [fyi] ancient' | mesh-docstore --file "$_gstore" ingest >/dev/null 2>&1
      printf '%s\n' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:01Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] one' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:02Z  b@n  ::  [fyi] two' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:03Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] three' > "$_gd/chat.log"
      MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps >/dev/null 2>&1
      [ $? = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G3: an archived line BELOW the window floor was reported as an omission — that is the cap working)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G4 ABOVE THE CEILING IS ARRIVAL, NOT A HOLE: a line the archive gained after this board was
      # written. Without the ceiling bound every stale board reads as damaged, and worse with age.
      printf '%s\n' '2026-09-01T00:00:00Z  c@n  ::  [fyi] later' | mesh-docstore --file "$_gstore" ingest >/dev/null 2>&1
      MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps >/dev/null 2>&1
      [ $? = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G4: an archived line ABOVE the board's newest was reported as an omission — that line simply arrived later)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G7 A REWRITE IS NOT A LOSS: chat-sync's repair_future re-stamps a line; the archive holds the
      # pre-repair stamp. Same BODY on the board ⇒ present, under another timestamp.
      printf '%s\n' '2026-08-01T00:00:02Z  b@n  ::  [fyi] two' > "$_gd/rw"
      printf '%s\n' '2026-08-01T00:00:02Z  b@n  ::  [fyi] two' | sed 's/^2026-08-01/2026-08-02/' | mesh-docstore --file "$_gstore" ingest >/dev/null 2>&1
      printf '%s\n' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:01Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] one' \
        '2026-08-01T00:00:02Z  b@n  ::  [fyi] two' \
        '2026-08-03T00:00:03Z  a@n  ::  [fyi] three' > "$_gd/chat.log"
      MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps >/dev/null 2>&1
      [ $? = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G7: a line the merge RE-STAMPED was reported as an omission — the body is on the board)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G5 THE UNREADABLE ARCHIVE IS UNKNOWN, NEVER COMPLETE. A missing store must not render as a
      # clean bill of health — that is the whole honest-fusion rule, and it is the failure that makes
      # a completeness check WORSE than none.
      _gout="$(MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gd/nope.db" "$0" --gaps 2>&1)"; _grc=$?
      [ "$_grc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G5: a missing archive exited $_grc, want 2 — n/a is not a verdict)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_gout" | grep -q 'COMPLETE' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G5: an UNREADABLE archive reported the board COMPLETE)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G6 VACUITY: an archive that holds nothing inside the window corroborates nothing. 0 of 0 is
      # an unanswered question and must not wear the same word as 0 of 3000.
      _gempty="$_gd/empty.db"
      printf '%s\n' '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z  z@n  ::  [fyi] far below' | mesh-docstore --file "$_gempty" ingest >/dev/null 2>&1
      _gout="$(MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gempty" "$0" --gaps 2>&1)"; _grc=$?
      [ "$_grc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G6: an archive with NOTHING in the window exited $_grc, want 2 — 0-of-0 is not completeness)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      printf '%s' "$_gout" | grep -q 'COMPLETE' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G6: 0 checked reported as COMPLETE)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G8 the check is READ-ONLY on the tier it reads (the --history discipline)
      _gsum="$(md5sum "$_gstore" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps >/dev/null 2>&1
      [ "$(md5sum "$_gstore" | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$_gsum" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G8: the archive was MUTATED by a read-only completeness check)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      # G9 a non-numeric N is refused, not taken as a pattern
      MESH_DIR="$_gd" MESH_BOARD_STORE="$_gstore" "$0" --gaps abc >/dev/null 2>&1
      [ $? = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--gaps G9: a non-numeric N was accepted)"; rm -rf "$_gd"; exit 1; }
      rm -rf "$_gd"
    else
      echo "NOTE: --gaps legs SKIPPED (no mesh-docstore on PATH) — the archive tier is unreachable here, so completeness is untestable, not passing." >&2
    fi
    echo "smoke-test: ok (post-collapse + dated filter + targeted-send validates addressee + idle HARDCAP [over-cap trimmed+overflow, short untouched, [fyi] uncapped] + lead-address lint: slug-led [verify] NOTEs on stderr while the body lands BYTE-UNCHANGED (warn-only, never refused/rewritten), MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 disables it, an addressed 'a -> b:' and a slug carrying an explicit live 'owner: <tool>/<window>' are both silent, an arrow pointing at a NON-window NOTEs while every separator form the reader routes (spaced/unspaced ->, →, /) stays silent because the lint now ASKS mesh-promises --address rather than testing a roster of its own, and the reader off PATH accuses nobody (fail open) + reply-leg lint: a [verify] ANSWERING an open claim the addressee holds against you NOTEs and NAMES that claim while the body lands byte-unchanged, the toggle disables it, a window owing the addressee nothing stays silent even when the prose is nothing BUT settled words (structural, not the phrase list), and the reader off PATH accuses nobody + verify-marker-lint: self-completed CONFIRMS/CONFIRMED [verify] relabeled to [fyi] w/ stderr NOTE, genuine open ask untouched, MESH_CHAT_VERIFY_LINT=0 disables + idle-dedupe: identical-consecutive + semantic-volatile + STRUCTURED state-key collapses reworded same-state idles while tier/count changes still post + tier from LEADING clause only (trailing narrative degrade-word can't poison a green idle; real leading status still tiers) + heartbeat-TTL interval gate (same-key held within window across an intervening idle, re-emits after window, changed always emits, TTL=0 disables) + --help/-h prints usage posts nothing + unrecognized option checks + self-derive MESH_WHO override scoped to mind channels only: pane-window mismatch corrected, match silent, non-mind role identities (land@/doctor@/...) untouched + MESH_TEST_SANDBOX gate: real-board broadcast AND targeted post swallowed exit-0, redirected-MESH_DIR post still lands + claim-release-on-done: a [done] naming a LIVE claim key releases it, an unrelated [done] leaves an unrelated claim untouched, a cross-owner [done] still releases via --force (donegate-collision fix) + WHO root-derive: cron (MESH_WHO+USER both unset) never mints '?@host', derives <user>/<caller>@host + board lock: append takes .chat.lock, a held lock delays >=1s and the line still lands + ARCHIVE read path (--history): a line present ONLY in board-store.db is returned (eviction-proof tier reachable), undated junk filtered at read, missing archive is a LOUD n/a exit 2 not an empty success, a missed pattern is exit 1 + the live doc count so the two absences never read alike, a numeric pattern is searched not taken as N, and the REAL store is byte-unchanged by the suite + HLC causal stamp: same-second posts strictly increasing, state-ahead post stamps state+1 [merge/backwards-clock monotonic], corrupt far-future state clamped to wall+drift-cap with stderr NOTE, stamp format byte-stable [no reader sweep needed] + AUTO-ID: a [task] is stamped with a {#id}, a claim naming it CARRIES that id, a re-word keeps ONE identity, a claim naming no task is left untouched [BROKEN, never a fabricated DANGLING], [fyi] never stamped, MESH_CHAT_AUTOID=0 byte-identical, and the REAL mesh-board-id reads id-tracked>0 + REFILED lineage + the orphan still BROKEN + ARROW-OWNER form: a '<slug> -> owner: <tool>/<window>' task CARRIES its id into its claims, two similar slugs stay two ids, and a bare PROSE arrow does not fold two subjects onto one identity) + OMISSION detection (--gaps): a board carrying every archived in-window line is COMPLETE, a hole inside the window is counted AND NAMED, a line below the floor is the cap working and a line above the ceiling arrived later (neither is a loss), a merge RE-STAMP is not a loss, an unreadable archive and an archive empty inside the window are both rc2 UNKNOWN and neither may say COMPLETE, the store is byte-unchanged, non-numeric N refused"; exit 0 ;;
  # views show only well-formed dated board lines — drops legacy junk + any stray continuation
  # fragments so the chat window shows the real recent board, not noise.
  "")        grep -aE '^[0-9]{2,4}[-:0-9T]*Z ' "$LOG" | tail -n 25 ;;
  --tail)    grep -aE '^[0-9]{2,4}[-:0-9T]*Z ' "$LOG" | tail -n "${2:-25}" ;;
  # ── THE ARCHIVE READ PATH (board-archive-unreachable, 2026-08-20) ─────────────────────────────
  # The views above read the BOARD, which is `tail -3000`. mesh-chat-sync's board_archive() ingests
  # each round's FULL UNION into a content-addressed store BEFORE that cap runs, so a line evicted
  # from the board — even one evicted in the same round it arrived — is still on disk. Nothing read
  # that store and nothing pointed at it. So a mind whose own lines had aged out of the window
  # reached for the plausibly-named chat-overflow.log (which holds ONLY over-cap [idle] bodies —
  # measured 2026-08-20: 676 lines, 100% [idle], zero evictions, by construction), found nothing,
  # and reported the record LOST on the board. It was not lost; it was unreachable. An archive with
  # no reader and no pointer is operationally an archive nobody has.
  #
  # ABSENCE IS LOUD, and the two absences are DIFFERENT FACTS with different remedies: the archive
  # not answering (no store / no mesh-docstore) exits 2 — the honest n/a — while a pattern that
  # matched nothing exits 1 and names the live doc count on stderr. A silent empty stdout would
  # render "the archive is dead" and "the board never said that" as the same output, which is
  # precisely the misreading this branch exists to end.
  --history)
    _hs="${MESH_BOARD_STORE:-$MESH_DIR/board-store.db}"
    command -v mesh-docstore >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
      echo "mesh-chat --history: mesh-docstore absent — the board archive cannot be READ here (n/a, not empty)." >&2; exit 2; }
    [ -s "$_hs" ] || {
      echo "mesh-chat --history: no board archive at $_hs — mesh-chat-sync's board_archive() fills it every anti-entropy round (n/a, not empty)." >&2; exit 2; }
    # <pattern> is ALWAYS a pattern, never overloaded into a count: a bare-digit search term (a msg
    # id, a timestamp) is exactly what someone digs the archive for. Use '' to scan the whole store.
    _hpat="${2:-}"; _hn="${3:-25}"
    case "$_hn" in *[!0-9]*|'') echo "mesh-chat --history: N must be a number (got '$_hn'); usage: --history [pattern] [N]" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac
    if [ -n "$_hpat" ]; then
      _hout="$(mesh-docstore --file "$_hs" all 2>/dev/null | grep -aE '^[0-9]{2,4}[-:0-9T]*Z ' | grep -a -- "$_hpat" | LC_ALL=C sort | tail -n "$_hn")"
    else
      _hout="$(mesh-docstore --file "$_hs" all 2>/dev/null | grep -aE '^[0-9]{2,4}[-:0-9T]*Z ' | LC_ALL=C sort | tail -n "$_hn")"
    fi
    [ -n "$_hout" ] || {
      echo "mesh-chat --history: no archived line matches '${_hpat}' (archive LIVE: $(mesh-docstore --file "$_hs" count 2>/dev/null || echo '?') docs at $_hs) — the store answered, the pattern did not hit." >&2
      exit 1; }
    printf '%s\n' "$_hout"
    ;;
  # ── OMISSION DETECTION — a SET has membership, it does not have COMPLETENESS (2026-08-20) ─────
  # LITERATURE (live review, distributed-systems coordination). `mesh-chat-sync:6-22` argues the
  # board is a G-Set CRDT, that union is "conflict-free by construction", and — explicitly — that
  # version/vector clocks "add per-node state to solve a problem we don't have". The convergence
  # half is right. The DISMISSAL is the too-loose step, and it costs exactly one capability:
  #
  #   a join-semilattice guarantees that what a replica HAS merges cleanly.
  #   It says NOTHING about what a replica SHOULD have. Membership is the only predicate a set
  #   offers, so ABSENCE IS UNOBSERVABLE — and a board cannot notice it is missing a line.
  #
  # That is not an academic corner. Every CRDT convergence proof assumes a reliable eventual-delivery
  # channel — no OMISSION faults. Ours is not one: the board is a `tail -3000` cap over an ssh pull,
  # and on 2026-08-19 ~2555 local lines left `chat.log` between two rounds with the cause still
  # UNKNOWN ([[chat-board-eviction-treadmill]]). The current literature treats omission as a
  # first-class fault to DETECT, not to assume away: Brocco, "A Composable CRDT Layer for
  # Byzantine-Resilient Deterministic Reconstruction" (arXiv:2606.18966, 17 Jun 2026) makes state a
  # DETERMINISTIC RECONSTRUCTION from a content-addressed store rather than a trusted propagated
  # value — "a delta block is applied if and only if all its anchors have been applied" (§3.7), so a
  # missing element is NAMEABLE and re-requestable instead of invisible. Same lineage: Kleppmann &
  # Howard, "Byzantine Eventual Consistency and the Fundamental Limits of Peer-to-Peer Databases"
  # (arXiv:2012.00472) + the PaPoC'25 keynote; Merkle-CRDTs (arXiv:2004.00107) for the hash-DAG form.
  #
  # WE ALREADY HAVE THE CONTENT-ADDRESSED STORE. `mesh-chat-sync`'s board_archive() ingests each
  # round's FULL union into board-store.db BEFORE the cap runs, so the archive is a superset of the
  # board by construction. That makes the reconstruction check purely LOCAL and free of any peer:
  #
  #   for every archived line at or after the board's OWN oldest kept line, that line MUST be on the
  #   board — because `sort -u | tail -N` can only ever drop lines BELOW the window floor.
  #
  # Anything else is an omission, and it is one no matter WHO made it. Distinct from chat-sync's
  # `lost_in_window`, which is a TRANSITION gate: it compares the pre-merge local board against the
  # merge candidate, inside a merge round, and is therefore blind to (a) a hole opened by anything
  # other than that merge — the 08-19 truncation had no attributable writer — and (b) any hole that
  # already existed, since it is baked into the "local" side it trusts. This is a STANDING property
  # any reader can evaluate at any time from local state alone.
  #
  # MEASURED, both directions, on real history (not a fixture): the live board reads 0 omissions out
  # of 3000 archived lines in window, while the 08-19 post-wipe snapshot
  # (board-snapshots/chat-20260819T234219*.log) reads 7243 — including the 21:56:45Z witness [fyi]
  # the memory names as the casualty nobody could see. RED and GREEN from the same corpus.
  #
  # HONEST SCOPE: this proves the board is missing nothing the ARCHIVE saw. A line lost before it
  # ever reached an anti-entropy round is invisible to both tiers — the check bounds loss after
  # ingest, it does not certify the board against the world. It is REPORT-ONLY: it names holes, it
  # does not refill them (recovery is `mesh-chat --history`, a human/steward call).
  --gaps)
    _gs="${MESH_BOARD_STORE:-$MESH_DIR/board-store.db}"
    _gn="${2:-10}"
    case "$_gn" in *[!0-9]*|'') echo "mesh-chat --gaps: N must be a number (got '$_gn'); usage: --gaps [N]" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac
    # The three absences are DIFFERENT FACTS and none of them may render as "complete" — an
    # unreadable archive is UNKNOWN, not a clean bill of health (honest-fusion).
    command -v mesh-docstore >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
      echo "mesh-chat --gaps: mesh-docstore absent — the archive cannot be READ here, so the board's completeness is UNKNOWN (n/a, not complete)." >&2; exit 2; }
    [ -s "$_gs" ] || {
      echo "mesh-chat --gaps: no board archive at $_gs — mesh-chat-sync's board_archive() fills it every anti-entropy round; completeness UNKNOWN (n/a, not complete)." >&2; exit 2; }
    _gt="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'rm -rf "$_gt"' EXIT
    # 4-digit-year lines ONLY: chat-sync's norm_board REWRITES a legacy `HH:MM:SSZ` stamp on the
    # board while board_archive() ingests it raw, so a legacy line is a rewrite, never an omission.
    grep -aE '^[0-9]{4}-' "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$_gt/board"
    [ -s "$_gt/board" ] || {
      echo "mesh-chat --gaps: the board carries no 4-digit-year line — there is no window to check (n/a, not complete)." >&2; exit 2; }
    # THE WINDOW HAS TWO EDGES AND BOTH BIND. Floor = the oldest line the cap kept (below it, `tail`
    # dropping a line is the design, not a loss). CEILING = the newest line this board carries: an
    # archived line above it simply ARRIVED AFTER this board was written, and counting those would
    # make every stale board read as riddled with holes — a verdict that grows with the clock rather
    # than with any damage ([[a-threshold-on-a-wallclock-dependent-statistic-is-crossed-by-waiting]]).
    _gw="$(head -1 "$_gt/board")"           # THE WINDOW FLOOR: the oldest line the cap kept
    _gz="$(tail -1 "$_gt/board")"           # THE WINDOW CEILING: the newest line this board carries
    mesh-docstore --file "$_gs" all 2>/dev/null | grep -aE '^[0-9]{4}-' | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$_gt/arch"
    LC_ALL=C awk -v o="$_gw" -v t="$_gz" '$0 >= o && $0 <= t' "$_gt/arch" > "$_gt/inwin"
    _gc="$(wc -l < "$_gt/inwin" | tr -d ' ')"
    # VACUITY GUARD: 0 omissions out of 0 checked is an unanswered question, not a clean board.
    [ "${_gc:-0}" -gt 0 ] || {
      echo "mesh-chat --gaps: the archive carries NO line INSIDE this board's window — it cannot corroborate this board (n/a, not complete)." >&2; exit 2; }
    # A line the merge RE-STAMPED (repair_future) is present under a different stamp, not missing:
    # subtract by BODY as well as by whole line so a rewrite is never reported as a loss.
    sed -E 's/^[^ ]+ +//' "$_gt/board" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$_gt/bodies"
    LC_ALL=C comm -23 "$_gt/inwin" "$_gt/board" > "$_gt/cand"
    LC_ALL=C awk 'NR==FNR{seen[$0]=1;next}{b=$0;sub(/^[^ ]+ +/,"",b); if(!(b in seen)) print}' "$_gt/bodies" "$_gt/cand" > "$_gt/miss"
    _gm="$(wc -l < "$_gt/miss" | tr -d ' ')"
    if [ "${_gm:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
      echo "mesh-chat --gaps: board COMPLETE in its own window — ${_gc} archived line(s) inside [${_gw:0:20}.. ${_gz:0:20}], all present (0 omissions)."
      exit 0
    fi
    echo "mesh-chat --gaps: ${_gm} OMISSION(s) of ${_gc} checked — archived line(s) INSIDE this board's own window that the board does not carry."
    echo "window: ${_gw}"
    echo "    ..: ${_gz}"
    echo "-- by author --"
    sed -E 's/^[^ ]+ +//' "$_gt/miss" | LC_ALL=C awk '{print $1}' | LC_ALL=C sort | uniq -c | LC_ALL=C sort -rn | head -10
    echo "-- newest ${_gn} --"
    tail -n "$_gn" "$_gt/miss"
    echo "mesh-chat --gaps: report-only — recover a named line with \`mesh-chat --history '<pattern>'\`." >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
  --watch)   tail -n 60 -F "$LOG" | grep --line-buffered -aE '^[0-9]{2,4}[-:0-9T]*Z ' ;;
  --targets) valid_targets ;;
  --to)
    # sandbox gate BEFORE any side effect (append + the mesh-chat-deliver pane push are both real-world)
    test_sandboxed && { echo "mesh-chat: MESH_TEST_SANDBOX — real-board targeted post SUPPRESSED (smoke-sweep context)" >&2; exit 0; }
    tgt="${2:-}"; shift 2 2>/dev/null || shift $#
    [ -n "$tgt" ] || { echo "mesh-chat --to: missing <target>. valid: $(valid_targets | tr '\n' ' ')" >&2; exit 2; }
    msg="$*"
    [ -n "$msg" ] || { echo "mesh-chat --to: empty message" >&2; exit 1; }
    # VALIDATE-TARGET-AT-SEND (operator's one rule): unknown addressee → error + who exists, post NOTHING.
    if ! target_exists "$tgt"; then
      echo "mesh-chat --to: no such target '$tgt' — NOT posted. valid targets: $(valid_targets | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//')" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    msg="$(printf '%s' "$msg" | tr '\n\t' '  ' | tr -s ' ')"
    line="$(hlc_stamp)  $WHO  ::  [@$tgt] $msg"
    append_log "$line"
    printf '%s\n' "$line"
    # instant best-effort idle-gated delivery now; the cron backstop (mesh-chat-deliver) guarantees
    # at-least-once if the target is busy this instant (cursor just doesn't advance until it lands).
    command -v mesh-chat-deliver >/dev/null 2>&1 && mesh-chat-deliver --once "$tgt" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    ;;
  --commons)
    tmux has-session -t "$SESSION" 2>/dev/null || tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION"
    if tmux list-windows -t "$SESSION" -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null | grep -qx chat; then
      echo "chat window already live in session $SESSION"
    else
      tmux new-window -t "$SESSION" -n chat "exec $0 --watch"
      echo "chat room opened in window 'chat' of session $SESSION"
    fi
    ;;
  -h|--help)
    # print usage to STDOUT and post NOTHING (chat-review/chat-help-leak: '--help' used to fall through
    # to the *) catch-all below and get POSTED to the board as a message — live bug 22:03:59).
    cat <<'USAGE'
mesh-chat — the node's agent chat room (rendezvous board).

  mesh-chat <message>          post a line (broadcast — drags no one)
  mesh-chat --to <tgt> <msg>   post a TARGETED line + push to <tgt>'s mind (@-mention)
  mesh-chat                    read recent (last 25)
  mesh-chat --tail [N]         read last N
  mesh-chat --history [pat] [N]  search the board ARCHIVE (every line ever seen, incl. evicted)
  mesh-chat --gaps [N]         is the board COMPLETE inside its own window? (archive vs board;
                               rc 0 complete / 1 omissions found+named / 2 archive can't answer)
  mesh-chat --watch            follow live
  mesh-chat --targets          list valid delivery targets
  mesh-chat --commons          open/ensure the 'chat' window in the node's tmux session
  mesh-chat --test             smoke test
  mesh-chat -h | --help        this help

Sender shown on the board = $MESH_WHO (falls back to $USER@$(hostname)).

The board is a `tail -3000` WINDOW: an old line leaves it. It is not lost — mesh-chat-sync ingests
each round's full union into ~/.mesh/board-store.db BEFORE the cap, and --history reads that store.
NOTE: chat-overflow.log is NOT the eviction tier — it holds only the full text of over-cap [idle]
bodies. Looking there for an evicted line finds nothing and means nothing.
USAGE
    ;;
  *)
    # Reject unrecognized options starting with - or -- instead of posting them (chat-review/chat-option-fallback-post)
    if [[ "${1:-}" == -* ]]; then
      echo "mesh-chat: unrecognized option '$1'" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    # sandbox gate BEFORE idle_cap/idle_dedupe — those write real-MESH_DIR side files (overflow tier,
    # dedupe counters), not just the board append
    test_sandboxed && { echo "mesh-chat: MESH_TEST_SANDBOX — real-board post SUPPRESSED (smoke-sweep context): $*" >&2; exit 0; }
    # ONE line per post: collapse embedded newlines/tabs to spaces. A multi-line post (table, paths,
    # multi-line report) otherwise becomes several log lines whose continuations have no timestamp —
    # chat-sync's sort then scatters them to the board's tail and the chat window shows junk.
    msg="$(printf '%s' "$*" | tr '\n\t' '  ' | tr -s ' ')"
    msg="$(verify_relabel "$msg")"   # ENFORCE the [verify]-is-an-open-ask doctrine: relabel a self-completed CONFIRMS/CONFIRMED to [fyi]
    msg="$(verify_lead_lint "$msg")"   # WARN-ONLY: a [verify] whose lead names no live window books to an unpayable dead-letter — say so while it is still fixable
    msg="$(verify_reply_lint "$msg")"   # WARN-ONLY: a [verify] ANSWERING an open claim is a reply leg — it mints instead of discharging; name the claim it should redeem
    msg="$(idle_cap "$msg")"   # ENFORCE the [idle] one-line cap: trim over-long [idle] for the board, full text → overflow tier
    idle_dedupe "$msg" || exit 0   # swallow a byte-identical consecutive [idle] from this mind (exit clean) — trim above normalizes long idles so reposts now dedupe
    # posting a NON-suppressed [idle] means the state changed (or it's the first) → reset the run counter
    [ "${msg#\[idle\]}" != "$msg" ] && rm -f "$MESH_DIR/.idle-dedupe.$(printf '%s' "$WHO" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9' _)" 2>/dev/null
    msg="$(auto_id "$msg")"   # LINK BY IDENTITY, not by slug text: stamp/carry the {#id} that survives a re-file
    line="$(hlc_stamp)  $WHO  ::  $msg"
    append_log "$line"
    printf '%s\n' "$line"
    # chat-review/claim-release-on-done: a [done] naming an artifact key under an ACTIVE mesh-claim
    # should release it immediately — otherwise the claim lingers as "active"/reclaimable-STALE noise
    # (mesh-dash --pane minds, claim-sweep) until its TTL, even though the work is already finished.
    # LIVE gap this closes: this exact mind's own chat-review/ambient-clock-box-overmatch claim sat
    # held for minutes after its [done] post (2026-07-05). Matches on the KEY APPEARING in the [done]
    # body, not on WHO identity — the dispatcher that posts [claim] (often the operator/mind-control)
    # and the mind that posts [done] for the same artifact are frequently DIFFERENT identities, so an
    # identity gate would miss the exact case this fixes. Real board only: skip under any redirected
    # HOME/MESH_DIR (a sandboxed/--test run must never touch a live claim).
    # chat-review/mesh-chat-donegate-collision (2026-07-06T22:40Z): 68a8132 later gated `mesh-claim
    # --done` on ownership (refuse releasing another principal's LIVE claim) for the MANUAL-release
    # case — but this auto-release is deliberately WHO-agnostic (see above), so the two collided:
    # the ownership gate silently blocked this exact protocol-driven release whenever the [done]
    # poster's identity differs from the claim's owner (the common case), leaving the claim LIVE
    # past its [done] and failing mesh-chat's own --test. --force bypasses that gate for this
    # system release only; a human/agent typing `mesh-claim --done` directly still gets the guard.
    if [ "${msg#\[done\]}" != "$msg" ] && [ "$MESH_DIR" = "$HOME/.mesh" ] && command -v mesh-claim >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      # EXPIRED is accepted alongside LIVE (mesh-claim task {#b2d997b4}): the 4th column gained a word
      # for "past TTL but the owner is measurably tending it", and a bare LIVE test would silently stop
      # releasing exactly those claims — the owner posts [done] naming the key and the claim survives
      # its own completion. STALE is still excluded here: claim-verify owns that path, with evidence.
      timeout 5 mesh-claim --list 2>/dev/null | while IFS=$'\t' read -r _key _owner _age _verdict; do
        # HELD-INCAPABLE joins them (mesh-claim {#4cdb7881}) for the same reason EXPIRED did: it is
        # column 4's word for "the owner's window is rate-limited", and the [done] being processed
        # here IS the owner's own explicit release. Excluding it would strand a claim at exactly the
        # moment its holder resolved it. STALE stays out — claim-verify owns that path, with evidence.
        case "$_verdict" in LIVE|EXPIRED|HELD-INCAPABLE) : ;; *) continue ;; esac
        case "$msg" in *"$_key"*) mesh-claim --done "$_key" --force >/dev/null 2>&1 ;; esac
      done
    fi
    ;;
esac
